window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "Quebec private law is often described as a mixed legal order because codified civil law and common law influences operate together within one legal system. Which statement best describes how a lawyer should understand these traditions when analysing a practical private law problem?", "options": {"A": "Private law follows English common law for contracts and ignores Quebec legislation", "B": "Civil law governs public administration while common law governs family relationships", "C": "Private law is mainly codified while public law reflects common law influences", "D": "The traditions are interchangeable labels with no effect on legal reasoning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Private law is mainly codified while public law reflects common law influences): Quebec's mixed order uses codified private law while common law ideas remain important in public law. A fails because private law is not governed by English common law without legislation. B is the strongest wrong option because it reverses the usual fields in which the two traditions are most prominent in Quebec. The practical consequence is that a lawyer must identify the legal field before choosing the governing tradition and sources."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "In current Quebec civil law, Civil Code article 6 uses the concept of good faith in the exercise of civil rights. Which statement best explains the scope of that duty for a person who holds a right and chooses how to use it during performance?", "options": {"A": "Good faith governs the manner in which a person exercises civil rights", "B": "Good faith applies only before a contract has been formed by the parties", "C": "Good faith replaces every express obligation contained in the agreement", "D": "Good faith applies only when the other party proves a financial loss"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Good faith governs the manner in which a person exercises civil rights): article 6 requires good faith in the exercise of civil rights and therefore reaches conduct after a right arises. B fails because good faith is not restricted to the period before contract formation. C is the strongest wrong option because good faith supplements and controls performance rather than replacing express obligations. The practical consequence is that the holder of a valid right may still be liable for exercising it improperly."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "A landlord exercises a contractual right to refuse a minor repair, but internal messages show that the purpose is to punish a tenant for requesting a safety inspection. The refusal is otherwise permitted by the lease. What claim is the tenant best positioned to raise?", "options": {"A": "The lease lacks an object because the landlord disliked the tenant", "B": "The tenant may ignore the lease because inspections are public law matters", "C": "The landlord exercised the right with intent to injure, contrary to good faith", "D": "The landlord's motive is irrelevant because the lease expressly permits refusal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The landlord exercised the right with intent to injure, contrary to good faith): article 7 prohibits exercising even an existing right with the intent of injuring another. A fails because dislike does not by itself remove the lease's legal object. D is the strongest wrong option because an express contractual permission does not authorize an abusive purpose. The practical consequence is that the tenant should seek a remedy for abuse rather than argue that the lease automatically vanishes."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "A merchant's website term conflicts with a mandatory protection in the Consumer Protection Act, while general contract rules appear in the Civil Code. The merchant argues that the Code alone governs every consumer transaction. What is the strongest legal response?", "options": {"A": "The Code generally displaces protective statutes because it is called general law", "B": "The consumer may select whichever statute produces the lower obligation", "C": "The protective statute can add or qualify the Code rules within its valid scope", "D": "The website term controls because electronic contracts cannot be regulated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The protective statute can add or qualify the Code rules within its valid scope): the Consumer Protection Act supplies mandatory protections that operate alongside general contract law in the Civil Code. A fails because the Code's general character does not erase a valid special protection. B is the strongest wrong option because a party cannot choose a statute according to which obligation it prefers. The practical consequence is that the website term must be tested against the protective statute and the Code together."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "In Quebec's current civil law tradition, judicial decisions apply codified rules but are not themselves enacted legislation. Which statement best describes the practical authority of prior judgments when a Quebec court considers a new private law dispute carefully in court?", "options": {"A": "They have no legal influence unless the legislature later incorporates them", "B": "They are not enacted rules but may be binding or strongly persuasive according to court level", "C": "They automatically have the same force as a section of the Civil Code", "D": "Only trial decisions matter because appellate judgments merely repeat facts, in this case."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They are not enacted rules but may be binding or strongly persuasive according to court level): Quebec judgments apply codified law, and their authority depends on the place of the deciding court and the issue. A fails because higher court decisions do influence later cases without statutory incorporation. C is the strongest wrong option because a judgment is not automatically equivalent to an enacted Civil Code article. The practical consequence is that counsel must verify the court level and the breadth of the earlier ruling."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "Judicial decisions, statutes, regulations, doctrine, and usage may all appear in current Quebec legal research. Which statement correctly explains the usual role of doctrine when a lawyer interprets and applies a Civil Code provision in a Quebec court applying the law?", "options": {"A": "It organizes and criticizes the law but cannot replace a valid statutory rule", "B": "It creates binding law whenever two respected authors reach the same conclusion", "C": "It applies only to public law and has no role in private law research", "D": "It overrides regulations whenever a textbook was published more recently"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It organizes and criticizes the law but cannot replace a valid statutory rule): doctrine explains and evaluates legal rules but remains secondary to legislation and binding judicial authority. B fails because agreement among authors does not enact law. C is the strongest wrong option because doctrine is used extensively in private law research. The practical consequence is that scholarly analysis can support interpretation while the current legislative text remains controlling."}, {"id": 23, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "A lease clause says that the lessor may inspect the premises at any reasonable time. The lessee argues that may creates no enforceable duty, while the lease also says inspections must occur twice yearly. How should the court read the word?", "options": {"A": "The word generally creates a strict duty despite the surrounding clauses", "B": "In context, may grants discretion while the other clause imposes timing", "C": "The word removes all inspection rights because permission is discretionary", "D": "The word must be ignored whenever the lease contains a schedule"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In context, may grants discretion while the other clause imposes timing): may usually expresses a power rather than a strict duty, but surrounding clauses can define how that power operates. A fails because may does not automatically create an absolute obligation. C is the strongest wrong option because discretion can still support an inspection right when the lease provides for inspections. The practical consequence is that both clauses must be read together without treating either word in isolation."}, {"id": 40, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "A litigant alleges a legal right and presents facts that appear credible and relevant to the underlying dispute. Why must a Quebec court still examine the legal elements of that right before granting the requested remedy, especially when the opposing party denies responsibility?", "options": {"A": "Credible facts replace the need to identify a legal rule or remedy", "B": "The burden shifts substantially once the claimant files the first exhibit", "C": "A court must grant a remedy when both parties agree that a dispute exists", "D": "The facts must satisfy each element that the governing rule requires"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The facts must satisfy each element that the governing rule requires): a plausible factual account matters only if it proves every element of the legal right and supports the remedy. A fails because facts cannot replace the governing rule. B is the strongest wrong option because one exhibit does not transfer all remaining proof obligations. The practical consequence is that the court denies the remedy when a necessary element remains unproved."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "A candidate reaches a result by saying the defendant was careless and therefore must pay the claimant financial compensation. Which statement best explains why the candidate should add the governing rule and the conditions for the requested remedy even when general fairness seems clear?", "options": {"A": "A short conclusion earns full marks when the facts suggest an unfair outcome", "B": "The remedy analysis is unnecessary whenever the word careless appears in the facts", "C": "A legal conclusion needs authority, factual proof, and a supported remedy", "D": "The governing rule matters only when the parties disagree about the facts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A legal conclusion needs authority, factual proof, and a supported remedy): carelessness does not by itself establish liability or the amount and form of compensation. A fails because an unfair appearance does not replace legal analysis. B is the strongest wrong option because a word in the facts cannot remove the remedy conditions. The practical consequence is that the answer must state the rule, apply each element, and justify the requested remedy."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "During commercial negotiations, one party promises to keep an offer open until Friday, then revokes it on Wednesday after learning that a better buyer exists. The written offer contains no expiry date. Which issue should a methodical legal analysis examine first?", "options": {"A": "Whether the later buyer's offer creates a binding public law obligation", "B": "Whether the promise attached a term preventing revocation before Friday", "C": "Whether the first offer automatically became a new regulation, in stated circumstances.", "D": "Whether Wednesday is shorter than Friday under the Civil Code"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Whether the promise attached a term preventing revocation before Friday): Article 1390 permits an offer to carry a term for acceptance and prohibits revocation before that term expires. The written offer's omission of a date does not by itself erase an express promise that attaches one, because the promise may form part of the parties' communications. If the promise validly attached the term, the Wednesday revocation was ineffective; if it did not, the offer could then be revoked before acceptance."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "civil_law_foundations_and_method", "question": "In Quebec's current civil law system, a judicial decision may state a general principle while a later case raises different facts. Which statement best explains how a lawyer should use the earlier judgment when advising a later court without treating it as a new statute?", "options": {"A": "Copy every sentence as binding legislation despite the facts", "B": "Compare the legal question, reasoning, court level, and material facts", "C": "Ignore the judgment unless the same parties appear again", "D": "Use the outcome only when it supports the client's desired remedy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compare the legal question, reasoning, court level, and material facts): a judgment's usefulness depends on the rule decided and the scope of the court's authority. A fails because a judgment is not literal legislation. C is the strongest wrong option because a precedent may influence cases involving different parties. The practical consequence is that counsel should identify similarities and differences before relying on the earlier ruling."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "persons", "question": "A 16 year old buys a winter coat and basic school supplies with money received from a part time job. The purchases are ordinary and match the teenager's needs and understanding. How should a current Quebec court assess the validity of these contracts?", "options": {"A": "Every purchase by a minor requires tutor representation before completion, under the stated transaction facts.", "B": "The parent must sign each purchase because the minor earns wages", "C": "The minor may contract alone for ordinary and usual needs within discernment", "D": "The merchant decides capacity by asking whether the coat is fashionable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The minor may contract alone for ordinary and usual needs within discernment): article 157 permits ordinary purchases suited to the minor's age and understanding. A fails because not every minor transaction requires representation. B is the strongest wrong option because earning wages does not remove the minor's existing power to make usual purchases. The practical consequence is that the coat and school supplies may form valid contracts if the factual limits are met."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "persons", "question": "Civil Code article 2 says that every person holds a patrimony. Which statement best explains what a patrimony includes and why current Quebec law limits its division or appropriation to a purpose when private rights and liabilities are involved before a court?", "options": {"A": "It concerns only physical property and excludes every debt owed by the person", "B": "It includes assets and liabilities and may be divided only as law allows", "C": "It may be divided freely by the person without statutory conditions or limits", "D": "It belongs only to persons who own an enterprise or operate a business"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It includes assets and liabilities and may be divided only as law allows): a patrimony is the legal whole of what a person owns and owes. A fails because debts are part of the patrimony. C is the strongest wrong option because article 2 does not permit unrestricted division or appropriation. The practical consequence is that property planning must respect the legal framework governing the person's assets and liabilities."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "persons", "question": "A patient discovers that a Quebec clinic keeps an inaccurate file about their health and refuses to correct it. The patient asks what rights the Civil Code provides for personal information kept by another person. Which answer best reflects the current file rules?", "options": {"A": "The person may seek access and correction of inaccurate or unjustified information", "B": "The clinic may refuse all access because health files belong only to the clinic", "C": "The person must buy a new health record from the registrar of civil status", "D": "The file rules apply only when the clinic publishes the information online, on established facts."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The person may seek access and correction of inaccurate or unjustified information): articles 37 to 40 govern the purpose, access, and correction of files kept on a person. B fails because the keeper does not own the personal information merely by holding the file. C is the strongest wrong option because the registrar's civil status register does not replace health file remedies. The practical consequence is that the patient can request access and correction subject to the Code's stated exceptions."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "persons", "question": "Current Civil Code article 10 protects inviolability and requires free and enlightened consent for interference with the person. Which statement best explains what enlightened consent requires before a Quebec health professional performs the proposed care on a patient under current Quebec rules?", "options": {"A": "The patient must sign a notarized form before every routine medical examination", "B": "The professional may infer consent from the patient's silence in all cases", "C": "The patient must accept every recommended treatment after receiving basic information", "D": "The patient must understand the proposed act and its material consequences"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The patient must understand the proposed act and its material consequences): free and enlightened consent requires meaningful information about the interference and its important effects. A fails because routine care does not require notarization. B is the strongest wrong option because silence is not generally adequate consent. The practical consequence is that the professional should provide information suited to the care and the patient's circumstances."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "persons", "question": "A Quebec business begins using the distinctive surname of a local artist as its brand, creating confusion about whether the artist endorses the products. The artist objects to the commercial use and seeks redress. Which civil status right most directly supports that objection under current law?", "options": {"A": "The right to change domicile without notifying the business registrar", "B": "The right to respect for one's name and redress for resulting confusion", "C": "The right to demand transfer of the business without proving misuse", "D": "The right to ignore all contracts because the brand use is commercial"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The right to respect for one's name and redress for resulting confusion): articles 55 and 56 protect the name and address misuse that causes confusion or injury. A fails because domicile is unrelated to commercial name use. C is the strongest wrong option because the artist does not automatically receive ownership of the business. The practical consequence is that the artist may object and seek redress if the misuse and its effects are proved."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "persons", "question": "A person needs official proof of their date of birth, parents, and marriage for a foreign application. The person asks the Quebec registrar for a document that records the relevant civil status particulars. Which document best matches article 146 under current law?", "options": {"A": "A private letter from a relative confirming the family details", "B": "A municipal tax bill showing the person's current address", "C": "A court judgment recognizing the foreign application form, under the stated consumer context.", "D": "A certificate of civil status containing the listed registry particulars"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A certificate of civil status containing the listed registry particulars): article 146 describes a certificate that states the person's name, birth particulars, parents, and applicable relationship and death information. A fails because a family letter lacks official status. B is the strongest wrong option because an address bill does not certify civil status. The practical consequence is that the registrar may issue the certificate only according to the access rules."}, {"id": 93, "domain": "persons", "question": "Support and parental authority serve different legal functions even when both concern a child. Which statement best distinguishes the support rule in article 585 from the rules governing custody, supervision, education, and related decision making for the child in a current family law dispute?", "options": {"A": "Support gives the creditor ownership of family property during the claim", "B": "Support addresses needs and means, while parental authority concerns care and decisions", "C": "Parental authority determines support once the child reaches full age", "D": "The two concepts are identical because both concern the family residence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Support addresses needs and means, while parental authority concerns care and decisions): article 585 provides support while articles 597 to 600 govern authority. A fails because support does not transfer property ownership. C is the strongest wrong option because parental authority is not the source of the support amount. The practical consequence is that a family dispute may require separate analysis of financial support and decision making."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "persons", "question": "Article 20 permits a capable adult to participate in research that may interfere with bodily integrity, but it adds conditions. Which statement correctly states the safeguards that apply to that research in current Quebec law when risk and ethics oversight are considered?", "options": {"A": "The participant may waive the safeguards after a private explanation, on supplied facts.", "B": "The research is permitted whenever the participant receives payment for inconvenience", "C": "The risk must be proportionate and an ethics committee must approve and monitor the project", "D": "Approval is needed only when the research uses a hospital operating room"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The risk must be proportionate and an ethics committee must approve and monitor the project): article 20 protects capable participants through a risk benefit assessment and ethics oversight. A fails because the participant cannot waive the statutory safeguards. B is the strongest wrong option because compensation does not remove the need for proportionate risk and approval. The practical consequence is that research may proceed only within the approved protocol and continuing ethics oversight."}, {"id": 114, "domain": "persons", "question": "A person poses a grave and immediate danger to others because of their mental state. Police bring the person to an institution before any court order, relying on the separate provincial statute for preventive confinement. Which rule explains that action?", "options": {"A": "Grave and immediate danger may permit preventive confinement without prior court authorization", "B": "Preventive confinement is available only after seven days of observation", "C": "The hospital must wait for a court order even when danger is immediate", "D": "A neighbour's written request can replace routine medical safeguards, for this analysis."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Grave and immediate danger may permit preventive confinement without prior court authorization): article 27 recognizes the separate preventive confinement law for grave and immediate danger. B fails because no seven day waiting period applies to that urgent route. C is the strongest wrong option because immediate danger can fall within the preventive confinement framework. The practical consequence is that the institution must comply with the statutory safeguards and medical requirements for preventive confinement."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "persons", "question": "A person applies to change their name because the current name invites ridicule and is too difficult to pronounce locally. Which authority may normally decide the administrative application, and what standard applies under article 58 and current Quebec law under this administrative route?", "options": {"A": "The municipality may authorize the change by a majority vote of residents", "B": "The court must review name requests before the registrar decides, under this present family context.", "C": "The registrar may authorize the change when a serious reason is established", "D": "A notary may decide the change if both parents provide consent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The registrar may authorize the change when a serious reason is established): article 58 gives the registrar authority over administrative name changes outside court jurisdiction. A fails because municipalities do not control civil status. B is the strongest wrong option because it reverses the normal administrative route. The practical consequence is that the applicant should provide the required reasons, notice, and supporting information to the registrar."}, {"id": 125, "domain": "persons", "question": "When a judgment changes a person's name or another essential status particular, the court process must communicate the result to the civil status registrar. Which statement best explains the clerk's duty under article 129 and current Quebec law after a status changing judgment?", "options": {"A": "The parties must carry the judgment to the registrar personally", "B": "The registrar learns of the change only through a newspaper notice", "C": "The judge must publish the judgment in the official gazette directly", "D": "The clerk gives notice of the final judgment to the registrar"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The clerk gives notice of the final judgment to the registrar): article 129 makes the clerk responsible for communicating a final judgment that alters status or a civil status particular. A fails because personal delivery by the parties is not the statutory route. B is the strongest wrong option because newspaper publication does not replace the clerk's notice. The practical consequence is that the registrar can then make the required entry or notation in the register."}, {"id": 137, "domain": "persons", "question": "The tutorship council has an ongoing role beyond advising on the initial appointment. Which statement best describes its general function in supervising a tutorship under article 222 and the related accounting rules in current Quebec law during the minor's tutorship?", "options": {"A": "It supervises the tutorship and provides decisions or advice required by law", "B": "It takes over ownership of the minor's property during the tutorship", "C": "It performs the Public Curator's accounting reviews in place of that office", "D": "It represents the tutor against the minor in later civil disputes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It supervises the tutorship and provides decisions or advice required by law): article 222 makes supervision the council's core function. B fails because the council does not acquire the minor's property. C is the strongest wrong option because the Public Curator retains statutory accounting functions. The practical consequence is that the council reviews the tutor's administration and acts when the law requires its decision or advice."}, {"id": 159, "domain": "persons", "question": "The tutorship council is separate from the tutor and serves several oversight functions. Which statement best summarizes its responsibilities concerning advice, accounts, and protection of the minor under the current Civil Code and Quebec law in a current tutorship file?", "options": {"A": "It manages the property directly and replaces the tutor in every transaction, under supplied legal framework.", "B": "It represents the minor in every lawsuit and becomes owner of the minor's assets", "C": "It supervises the tutor, reviews accounts, and gives decisions or advice required by law", "D": "It chooses the minor's school and residence while leaving property oversight to others"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It supervises the tutor, reviews accounts, and gives decisions or advice required by law): articles 222 and 233 describe the council's supervisory and decision making role. A fails because the council does not directly manage every transaction. B is the strongest wrong option because the council neither becomes owner nor automatically represents the minor in every case. The practical consequence is that the council checks tutorship administration while the tutor performs the daily duties."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "persons", "question": "A minor may have greater contractual freedom in some employment and professional settings. Which statement correctly describes the capacity of a minor aged fourteen or over for acts connected with employment, a craft, or a profession under Quebec article 156?", "options": {"A": "The minor must obtain tutor authorization for every employment contract", "B": "The minor may act only with the written approval of the tutorship council", "C": "The minor has no employment capacity until reaching full age", "D": "The minor is deemed full age for acts pertaining to that work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The minor is deemed full age for acts pertaining to that work): article 156 gives a minor aged fourteen or over full age capacity for employment, craft, or profession acts. A fails because tutor authorization is not required for every such contract. B is the strongest wrong option because the Code does not impose council approval on all work acts. The practical consequence is that the minor may manage the ordinary legal acts connected with the employment."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "persons", "question": "A 16 year old wants simple emancipation without the tutor filing the declaration, but the circumstances justify asking a court to grant it. The tutor and tutorship council have been informed and provide their views. What route does article 168 allow?", "options": {"A": "The minor must wait until age 17 before any court may act", "B": "The court may emancipate only if both parents make the application", "C": "The Public Curator alone may grant emancipation after an interview", "D": "The minor may apply alone for court granted simple emancipation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The minor may apply alone for court granted simple emancipation): article 168 allows a minor to request judicial emancipation after the required advice. A fails because no age seventeen waiting rule appears. B is the strongest wrong option because both parents do not need to make the application. The practical consequence is that the court may grant simple emancipation when the legal conditions and the minor's interests support it."}, {"id": 171, "domain": "persons", "question": "A declaratory judgment of death is issued after the statutory conditions are met even though no body has been produced. Which statement correctly describes the legal effect of that judgment under article 95 and current Quebec law in a Quebec estate?", "options": {"A": "It creates only a police record and has no civil effect", "B": "It produces the same effects as death for civil law purposes", "C": "It dissolves only debts but leaves status and succession unchanged", "D": "It takes effect only if the body is later discovered and identified"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It produces the same effects as death for civil law purposes): article 95 gives the declaratory judgment the effects of death. A fails because the judgment affects civil status and succession rather than only police records. C is the strongest wrong option because the effect is not limited to debts. The practical consequence is that parties must address the resulting status, family, and succession consequences under the Code."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "persons", "question": "Quebec law allows a person whose gender identity differs from the sex designation in the act of birth to seek a change. Which statement correctly reflects the current rule in article 71 about medical treatment and the application conditions in Quebec?", "options": {"A": "Surgery is required before the registrar may accept an application", "B": "The court must order medical treatment before any change is recorded", "C": "The change cannot depend on having undergone treatment or surgery", "D": "Only a person under eighteen may request the designation change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The change cannot depend on having undergone treatment or surgery): article 71 expressly protects that rule while setting other conditions through the Code and regulation. A fails because surgery is not a lawful prerequisite. B is the strongest wrong option because the court does not order medical treatment as part of the designation process. The practical consequence is that eligibility must be assessed without imposing a surgical or medical requirement."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A spouse refuses to consent to a needed family transaction, and the refusal is not justified by the family's interest. The other spouse cannot obtain consent and asks whether the court can allow the transaction. What does article 399 authorize in this current dispute?", "options": {"A": "The court may lastingly transfer all authority to one spouse, under relevant provisions.", "B": "The court may issue a special, time limited authorization for the act", "C": "The court may appoint the contracting party as family representative", "D": "The court must wait for the tutorship council to decide the issue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court may issue a special, time limited authorization for the act): Article 399 authorizes the court to allow one spouse to enter into the particular act requiring the other spouse's consent when consent is unavailable or the refusal is not justified by the family interest. The authorization is special and for a specified time and may be amended or revoked. It does not appoint the contracting spouse as a family representative or transfer general authority over the other spouse."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "For years, one spouse handled the family home and children without salary while the other built a successful company and retained most assets in that spouse's name. At divorce, the unpaid contribution has not been fully reflected in the matrimonial regime. Which remedy is designed for this enrichment under article 427?", "options": {"A": "A penalty requiring the company to transfer all shares to the state", "B": "A compensatory allowance for the contribution to the other spouse's enrichment", "C": "A criminal claim based on unpaid household labor after divorce", "D": "An automatic gift of the family residence to the unpaid spouse"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A compensatory allowance for the contribution to the other spouse's enrichment): article 427 allows compensation for a contribution to the other spouse's patrimonial enrichment when the regime and contract do not fully address it. A fails because the remedy is civil compensation rather than a state share transfer. C is the strongest wrong option because unpaid family work is not a criminal offence. The practical consequence is that the court assesses the contribution, enrichment, advantages, and payment method."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A seventeen year old wants to marry in Quebec while still subject to parental authority. The other intended spouse is an adult, and both understand the legal consequences of the ceremony. The couple asks the officiant which signature or approval will make the wedding lawful. What additional authorization does the minor ordinarily need?", "options": {"A": "Written approval from two municipal councillors before the wedding ceremony", "B": "A declaration signed by the officiant alone before the wedding", "C": "A permit from the registrar of civil status", "D": "A court must authorize the marriage of a minor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A court must authorize the marriage of a minor): the officiant must confirm court authorization before solemnizing the marriage of a minor. The minor may apply personally, while the person with parental authority must be summoned to give advice. B is the strongest wrong option because the officiant cannot authorize the marriage alone. The practical consequence is that the ceremony should not proceed until judicial authorization is obtained."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "One spouse buys ordinary household supplies for the family while the couple is not separated from bed and board. The supplier asks whether the other spouse can also be responsible. The purchase is for ordinary household needs and the couple still shares a common life. Which rule determines that liability?", "options": {"A": "The noncontracting spouse is protected in every case, under the supplied transaction facts.", "B": "The other spouse may be bound unless timely notice prevents liability", "C": "Only the spouse who signed the invoice owes money", "D": "Household purchases create no obligation for either spouse"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The other spouse may be bound unless timely notice prevents liability): a contract for ordinary family needs can bind both spouses during the marriage. The noncontracting spouse avoids that liability by informing the supplier before the contract that no liability is accepted. A is the strongest wrong option because protection does not arise automatically after a purchase. The practical consequence is that notice must reach the supplier before the credit is granted."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A married couple rents a dwelling and has notified the lessor that it is their family residence. The spouse named on the lease wants to transfer the lease to a third person. The other spouse asks what protection applies before the transfer becomes final and effective. Which requirement protects the other spouse?", "options": {"A": "The lessor may decide the transfer without consulting anyone", "B": "Written consent is required after the lessor receives family residence notice", "C": "The nontenant spouse has no protection concerning the lease", "D": "The couple's children must approve every lease transfer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Written consent is required after the lessor receives family residence notice): once the lessor knows the dwelling is the family residence, the tenant spouse cannot transfer the lease alone. Subletting and termination are subject to the same protection. A is the strongest wrong option because the lessor's decision does not replace the other spouse's written consent. The practical consequence is that the nonconsenting spouse may seek annulment of the transfer."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A married spouse owns a duplex containing fewer than five dwellings, and the family lives in one unit. The owner plans to grant a hypothec over the building without the other spouse's agreement. The other spouse lives in the duplex and has never agreed to the planned financing. Which consent rule applies?", "options": {"A": "No consent applies because the owner holds registered title", "B": "Consent is required only when the building has tenants", "C": "The municipal assessor may authorize the transaction after reviewing the building file", "D": "Written consent is required for an alienation, charge, or family dwelling lease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Written consent is required for an alienation, charge, or family dwelling lease): the rule protects an owner's immovable containing fewer than five dwellings when it serves as the family residence. A declaration of family residence can affect the remedies available against third persons. A is the strongest wrong option because registered ownership does not remove the protection. The practical consequence is that the transaction should not proceed without written consent or judicial authorization."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A married couple separates, and one spouse wants to remain in the rented family residence with the children. The lease is currently held by the other spouse. The other spouse objects and argues that only the named tenant can control the lease. Which power does the court have during the family law proceeding?", "options": {"A": "The court may award the lease, use, or ownership after marriage ends", "B": "The court may assign the children as lease guarantors", "C": "The court may change the building's municipal zoning, under the supplied remedy analysis.", "D": "The court may cancel every right of the lessor automatically"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court may award the lease, use, or ownership after marriage ends): family residence remedies can adjust housing after separation, divorce, or nullity. The court may impose conditions and an equalizing payment. D is the strongest wrong option because the court does not erase the lessor's lawful rights. The practical consequence is that the spouse seeking the home should present evidence about the children's needs, housing, and financial capacity."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A couple asks whether their choice of separation as to property prevents any shared treatment of the family home, car, and household furnishings. They want to understand the purpose of family patrimony. They want to know whether a property regime can remove this family protection. Which description is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is an optional trust created by the spouses, in this proceeding.", "B": "It applies only when a spouse dies without a will", "C": "Marriage creates a mandatory pool of designated family property", "D": "It is another name for the partnership of acquests"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Marriage creates a mandatory pool of designated family property): family patrimony operates because of the marriage and cannot be created merely by private choice. It must be distinguished from the matrimonial property regime. D is the strongest wrong option because family patrimony is not another name for partnership of acquests. The practical consequence is that separation as to property does not remove every family patrimony right."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "One spouse brought a valuable condominium into the marriage, and the property later became the family residence. The other spouse asks whether the full current value is divided equally. The spouses disagree about whether inclusion automatically requires equal division of the entire value. Which adjustment can recognize the property's premarital value?", "options": {"A": "The premarital property is mainly excluded from every calculation", "B": "The spouse who did not own it automatically receives the whole residence", "C": "Net value owned at marriage may be deducted from the share", "D": "The mortgage lender determines the spouses' equal shares"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Net value owned at marriage may be deducted from the share): the deduction prevents a spouse from sharing the value already owned before marriage. Proportional treatment may apply to increases in value. A is the strongest wrong option because the property can remain in the pool while generating deductions. The practical consequence is that the original net value and gross value should be proved with historical records."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "Five years after a Quebec wedding, a relative discovers that one spouse was already validly married when the ceremony occurred. The first marriage had not ended. The relative now seeks a declaration that the second marriage is null. How should the court treat the ordinary three year period?", "options": {"A": "The period bars every application after the fifth year", "B": "Only the first spouse may apply after that delay, under the described legal framework.", "C": "The delay validates the second marriage for both spouses", "D": "The prior marriage engages public order and overrides the ordinary time limit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The prior marriage engages public order and overrides the ordinary time limit): an existing marriage prevents formation of a valid new marriage. Public order can take the case outside the ordinary limitation. C is the strongest wrong option because later time does not validate a marriage affected by the prior bond. The practical consequence is that the interested person may ask the court to examine the nullity despite the delay."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "Under partnership of acquests, a spouse owns drafting equipment needed for a professional practice. The equipment was purchased during marriage, but the other spouse says every item bought then is an acquest. How should the equipment be classified under the general rule?", "options": {"A": "It becomes an acquest because it was bought during marriage", "B": "It belongs half to each spouse from the purchase date", "C": "Necessary work instruments remain private property, subject to possible compensation", "D": "It becomes family patrimony because it supports the household, under enacted rules."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Necessary work instruments remain private property, subject to possible compensation): the Code treats tools required for a spouse's occupation as private property. The private classification may require compensation when acquests financed the acquisition. A is the strongest wrong option because the purchase date alone does not make every item an acquest. The practical consequence is that the equipment's source of funds and any resulting compensation should be calculated."}, {"id": 252, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A married spouse has separated from the other spouse but remains legally married. The separated spouse now becomes a parent with a new de facto partner and wants to know when a parental union can form. Which timing rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The parental union forms as soon as the new household begins", "B": "The parental union forms after one year of continuous cohabitation", "C": "The parental union forms when the child is registered at school", "D": "The new union forms only after the existing marriage is dissolved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The new union forms only after the existing marriage is dissolved): a person already married cannot form a parental union with a new partner before that marriage ends. Separation alone does not dissolve the marriage bond. A is the strongest wrong option because beginning a new household does not remove the existing legal bond. The practical consequence is that the date the prior marriage ends must be established before the new parental union begins."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A spouse buys property using some private funds and some acquest funds. The private contribution is greater than half of the total acquisition cost. The spouses ask how partnership of acquests classifies the resulting property. They have receipts showing each contribution to the purchase price. Which rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The property is private, subject to compensation for the acquest contribution", "B": "The property becomes an acquest because some acquest funds were used", "C": "The property belongs to the lender financing the purchase", "D": "The property becomes family patrimony without further analysis, under the described case record."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The property is private, subject to compensation for the acquest contribution): property financed with private and acquest funds is private when the private contribution exceeds one half of the total cost. The acquest mass then receives compensation for its contribution. B is the strongest wrong option because the larger private contribution changes the classification. The practical consequence is that the spouse claiming private ownership must prove each source and amount."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A marriage is declared null after one spouse acted in bad faith while the other spouse relied honestly on the ceremony. The couple asks how gifts made in consideration of marriage are treated for each spouse. Which consequence can follow from bad faith?", "options": {"A": "The bad faith spouse automatically receives all marriage gifts", "B": "Every gift remains valid despite the spouses' conduct during the marriage", "C": "Only the officiant may decide whether gifts lapse", "D": "The bad faith spouse may lose gifts made in consideration of marriage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The bad faith spouse may lose gifts made in consideration of marriage): nullity can affect gifts when one spouse acted in bad faith. The court may also lapse, reduce, or defer certain gifts according to the circumstances. A is the strongest wrong option because bad faith does not improve the spouse's gift position. The practical consequence is that good faith and the nature of each gift should be proved during the nullity proceeding."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "One spouse regularly contributed to the other spouse's business and now claims compensation after the contributing spouse dies. The surviving spouse will also receive advantages from the deceased spouse's succession. The surviving spouse disputes the claim and asks which surrounding benefits matter. Which factors may the court consider?", "options": {"A": "Only the surviving spouse's new employment income after the death is relevant", "B": "Only the value of the family furniture at the final valuation date", "C": "The succession advantages and the matrimonial regime benefits", "D": "Only whether the business had a public website"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The succession advantages and the matrimonial regime benefits): the court considers the benefits the claimant receives from the succession after death. It also considers advantages produced by the matrimonial regime and marriage contract. A is the strongest wrong option because later employment income is not the only relevant factor. The practical consequence is that succession documents and the full property outcome should be presented."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A spouse helps operate the other spouse's catering company for eight years without salary. The owners sell the company while the marriage continues, ending that cooperation. The helper wants a compensatory allowance now rather than waiting for divorce. What is the correct result?", "options": {"A": "The helper must wait for one spouse's death, under the described consumer context.", "B": "The helper must wait until the marriage reaches ten years", "C": "The helper may request the allowance when cooperation ends through the sale", "D": "The helper may claim only a salary from the purchaser"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The helper may request the allowance when cooperation ends through the sale): regular cooperation in an enterprise can support a compensatory allowance. The claim may arise when the sale ends that cooperation even before divorce. A is the strongest wrong option because the death of a spouse is not the required trigger. The practical consequence is that the helper should document the duration and value of the contribution."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A spouse takes an expensive vehicle from the acquest mass and gives it to a friend without the other spouse's consent. The vehicle is not a small or customary present. The giving spouse argues that personal administration allows the transfer. Which result follows?", "options": {"A": "The transfer is valid because the friend accepted the vehicle", "B": "The other spouse's consent was required for this valuable gift", "C": "The transfer becomes valid when the vehicle is insured", "D": "The transfer is valid because the giving spouse possessed the keys"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The other spouse's consent was required for this valuable gift): an acquest cannot be given away without the other spouse's consent unless it is small or customary. The recipient's acceptance does not replace that missing consent. A is the strongest wrong option because acceptance cannot cure the lack of authority. The practical consequence is that the transaction may be challenged and the property value accounted for."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "Married spouses disagree about how to exercise a family right connected to the household's direction. They cannot resolve the dispute themselves and ask what role a court can perform. The disagreement affects decisions for themselves and their children. Which approach follows the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The court may decide in the family interest after encouraging conciliation", "B": "The court must dismiss every family dispute without hearing evidence", "C": "The court may transfer family authority to a municipal officer", "D": "The court must terminate the marriage before deciding, under the governing dispute record."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court may decide in the family interest after encouraging conciliation): a court can resolve a disagreement about rights and duties connected to family direction. The decision must serve the family's interest after an effort to encourage agreement. C is the strongest wrong option because a municipal officer does not receive judicial family authority. The practical consequence is that the spouses should present the family needs and evidence supporting each proposed arrangement."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A spouse asks for spousal support and describes misconduct that occurred during the marriage. The other spouse also submits financial evidence about needs and means. The court asks whether the misconduct can influence the support amount. How does the court treat the described misconduct?", "options": {"A": "Misconduct increases support in proportion to its seriousness", "B": "Misconduct ends the other spouse's right to present evidence", "C": "Misconduct in relation to the marriage is not considered", "D": "Misconduct determines the division of the family patrimony"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Misconduct in relation to the marriage is not considered): the Divorce Act excludes marital misconduct from the spousal support analysis. The court focuses on condition, means, needs, other circumstances, and the statutory objectives. A is the strongest wrong option because misconduct does not increase support according to its seriousness. The practical consequence is that the financial evidence should address need, ability, economic disadvantage, and caregiving consequences."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "Spouses who resumed a shared community of life after filing for separation ask what happens to the pending proceeding. They truly reconciled and no new application has been filed. The court has not yet rendered judgment. Which procedural result follows?", "options": {"A": "Reconciliation terminates the pending separation proceeding before the court", "B": "The proceeding continues because the original application was filed", "C": "The court must convert the application into a divorce", "D": "The reconciliation creates a new matrimonial property regime, under the governing transaction facts."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reconciliation terminates the pending separation proceeding before the court): a genuine reconciliation after the application ends the separation proceeding. A new application may later be based on grounds arising after the reconciliation. B is the strongest wrong option because the original application does not continue afterward. The practical consequence is that the date and reality of reconciliation should be documented."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A minor authorized to marry wants to sign a marriage contract giving and adjusting property rights. The minor asks whether the court must approve the agreement before the ceremony. The intended contract contains substantial property clauses, and the person with parental authority supports the marriage. Which rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The minor may sign alone because marriage authorizes every contract", "B": "The court must authorize the minor's matrimonial agreements", "C": "Only the other spouse must approve the contract", "D": "The officiant must draft and approve the agreement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court must authorize the minor's matrimonial agreements): a minor authorized to marry still needs court approval for matrimonial agreements in the marriage contract. The person with parental authority or the tutor must be summoned to give advice. A is the strongest wrong option because marriage authorization does not authorize every property agreement. The practical consequence is that the court application should present the proposed contract and the required advice."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A person of full age under tutorship wants to make a matrimonial agreement before marriage. The tutor asks what assistance or authorization is required before the agreement can bind the protected person. The matter concerns several property choices. Which rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The protected person may act alone without assistance", "B": "The bank must approve every clause in the matrimonial contract", "C": "The future spouse controls the protected person's rights", "D": "The tutor assists and may need court authorization with advice"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The tutor assists and may need court authorization with advice): a person of full age under tutorship needs prescribed assistance to make matrimonial agreements. The tutor or mandatary may need court authorization after advice from the tutorship council. A is the strongest wrong option because the protected person cannot act alone in that situation. The practical consequence is that the agreement and the required protective steps should be documented before it binds the person."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "family_and_matrimonial_regimes", "question": "A notary executes a marriage contract that changes an earlier contract previously held by a depositary. The notary asks what step connects the new act to the earlier record. The change may affect later copies of the contract. Which duty applies?", "options": {"A": "The notary may keep the change confidential from the depositary", "B": "The spouses must publish the change in a newspaper", "C": "The notary must notify the depositary so the change is noted", "D": "The earlier contract must be destroyed before registration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The notary must notify the depositary so the change is noted): a notary who changes an earlier marriage contract must immediately notify the depositary of the original and any changing contract. The depositary records the date, notary, and minute number of the change. A is the strongest wrong option because the depositary needs notice to keep the record accurate. The practical consequence is that the earlier contract and its copies should show the later change."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "Spouses separated on June first and file for divorce in November, before one year has passed. They remain apart and the court will not decide the case until the following June. The spouses ask whether early filing creates a fatal defect. What result is possible under the separation ground?", "options": {"A": "The application must be dismissed because it was filed early", "B": "The spouses must start a second proceeding in June, under the governing rights analysis.", "C": "The court may decide in June after the full year is complete", "D": "The court may grant a divorce in November immediately"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court may decide in June after the full year is complete): the application can begin before one year of separation has passed. The one year period must be complete when the court determines the divorce. A is the strongest wrong option because early filing does not require dismissal. The practical consequence is that the spouses should prove continuous separation from June first through the judgment date."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "Divorcing parents both live in Quebec when the support application is made. In another case, one parent lives in British Columbia. The lawyer asks which child support rules ordinarily apply in each scenario. The parents' habitual residences differ in the second example. Which answer is accurate?", "options": {"A": "The federal rules apply in both of the described situations", "B": "The Quebec rules apply in both of the described situations", "C": "Neither jurisdiction may calculate child support during a divorce, under the governing remedy analysis.", "D": "Both in Quebec uses the provincial scheme, while different provinces use federal rules"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Both in Quebec uses the provincial scheme, while different provinces use federal rules): the applicable guideline depends on the parents' habitual residence when the application is made. Both in the same designated province normally brings the provincial scheme into play, while different provinces normally bring the Federal Guidelines. A is the strongest wrong option because the federal rules are not automatic when both parents reside in Quebec. The practical consequence is that the residence facts must be recorded before calculating support."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A court is assessing a parenting order and considers the child's care history, developmental needs, relationships, and current arrangements. The parents ask which factors belong to the best interests analysis. They want to know whether the court may examine more than income. Which description is correct?", "options": {"A": "Only the parents' incomes and work schedules matter, under the governing consumer context.", "B": "Only the child's preference determines the final order", "C": "The court considers one factor chosen by each parent", "D": "History of care and the child's needs are among relevant factors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (History of care and the child's needs are among relevant factors): the best interests standard uses the child's full circumstances. The statutory list includes development, relationships, care history, views, heritage, safety, and cooperation. A is the strongest wrong option because parental income is not the only relevant consideration. The practical consequence is that the court should make findings on every material child centered factor."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A parent describes a pattern of threats and control by the other parent, none of which led to criminal charges. The parent asks whether those facts count as family violence in a parenting case. The parent wants to understand the federal definition used in parenting cases. Which definition is correct?", "options": {"A": "Family violence requires a criminal conviction before it counts", "B": "Coercive and controlling behaviour can be family violence without a crime", "C": "Only physical injury can qualify as family violence", "D": "Family violence concerns property damage and no other form of conduct"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Coercive and controlling behaviour can be family violence without a crime): the federal definition does not depend on a criminal conviction. It includes threats, harassment, psychological abuse, financial abuse, and patterns of control. A is the strongest wrong option because criminal charges are not required. The practical consequence is that the court should examine the pattern, its effect on the family, and any resulting safety concerns."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A court is assessing allegations of family violence in a parenting dispute. It considers frequency, seriousness, fear for safety, and the child's exposure. The parties ask what else the court should examine beyond physical injuries. Which additional factor is relevant to the statutory analysis?", "options": {"A": "The cash value of the former family home", "B": "The parents' preferred vacation destinations during the marriage", "C": "The number of photographs in the family album", "D": "Whether there is a pattern of coercive control"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Whether there is a pattern of coercive control): the statutory family violence analysis examines the nature, seriousness, frequency, timing, and effect of the conduct. A pattern of control is expressly relevant even when individual acts are not physical. A is the strongest wrong option because property value does not describe the pattern or safety risk. The practical consequence is that the evidence should address the course of conduct, the child's exposure, and the parties' safety."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "Parents ask which costs can be claimed beyond the basic federal table amount as special or extraordinary expenses. Their list includes childcare, medical costs, and postsecondary education. They want to know whether those costs fit the statutory list. Which category is included under section seven?", "options": {"A": "Specified childcare, health, education, and extraordinary activity costs", "B": "Every ordinary grocery and utility bill without qualification", "C": "Only the parents' personal travel expenses during the marriage", "D": "Only expenses approved by the child's school principal, under this disputed dispute record."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Specified childcare, health, education, and extraordinary activity costs): section seven lists categories that may be claimed beyond the basic table amount. Childcare, insurance, health costs, education, and extraordinary activities are examples. B is the strongest wrong option because ordinary household bills are not automatically special expenses. The practical consequence is that each expense must fit a listed category and satisfy the statutory tests."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A child support order already exists and the paying spouse's income changes from year to year. The receiving spouse asks whether updated financial information can be requested after the order. The request will be limited to once each year. Which federal rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The order lastingly freezes the income stated at trial", "B": "Updated income may be requested annually while the child qualifies", "C": "Only a new divorce application can reveal later income", "D": "The employer must send updates without any request"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Updated income may be requested annually while the child qualifies): the federal guidelines create a continuing disclosure process after a child support order. A spouse may request current documents on a limited annual basis. A is the strongest wrong option because the original income figure does not remain frozen forever. The practical consequence is that the receiving spouse should make a written request and preserve proof of delivery."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A spouse helped raise the other spouse's child for years, attended school meetings, provided care, and treated the child as part of the family. After divorce, the stepparent argues that support is impossible without adoption. What result is most likely?", "options": {"A": "The stepparent may owe support if the child qualifies federally", "B": "The stepparent owes nothing because the other biological parent exists", "C": "The stepparent owes support only if the child inherits the home", "D": "The stepparent owes support only after a second marriage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The stepparent may owe support if the child qualifies federally): a person who stood in the place of a parent can be within the federal support definition. The court examines the relationship, care, financial involvement, and the child's circumstances. B is the strongest wrong option because another parent's existence does not remove the stepparent issue. The practical consequence is that the child's relationship with the stepparent should be documented before the support claim is decided."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "Under the existing order, the child spends nearly all time with one parent, who now proposes relocation. The other parent objects and the court applies the burden rule. The parents disagree about who must present the main evidence. Which party must prove whether the move serves the child?", "options": {"A": "The relocating parent bears the burden in this majority arrangement", "B": "The child's school principal bears the burden of proof", "C": "The relocating parent's new employer bears the burden", "D": "The parent opposing relocation bears the burden of proof"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The parent opposing relocation bears the burden of proof): when the child spends the vast majority of time with the relocating parent, the objecting parent must show that relocation would not serve the child. The court still applies all general and additional best interests factors. A is the strongest wrong option because the relocating parent bears that burden in a substantially equal arrangement. The practical consequence is that the opposing parent should present evidence explaining why the move would harm the child."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "One parent exercises 65 percent of the child's parenting time, and the other parent exercises 35 percent. The majority-time parent incurs necessary child care because of employment, and both parents agree that the expense is reasonable. How should that child-care expense be handled under the federal guidelines?", "options": {"A": "The cost is ignored because shared parenting excludes special expenses", "B": "The cost is assigned mainly to the higher earning parent", "C": "The necessary cost may be shared in proportion to the parents' incomes", "D": "The cost is paid by the child's school without parental contribution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The necessary cost may be shared in proportion to the parents' incomes): section 7(1)(a) applies because the parent who incurred the child care has more than 60 percent of parenting time and the care results from employment. The expense must also be necessary and reasonable, considering the child's best interests and each spouse's financial circumstances. The remaining cost is ordinarily shared in proportion to income after eligible contributions, subsidies, benefits, deductions, and credits are considered. A is wrong because the majority-time structure here does not turn the matter into the section 9 shared-parenting analysis."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A parent requests reimbursement for an unusually expensive optional activity that the family could not afford before separation. The other parent argues the cost is not necessary or reasonable. The parents have receipts and evidence of their prior budget. What result is likely?", "options": {"A": "The entire expense must be paid because the child enjoys it", "B": "The court may refuse or reduce the expense under the reasonableness test", "C": "The activity becomes part of the basic table amount automatically, under this disputed succession context.", "D": "The other parent must pay because the request was made in writing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court may refuse or reduce the expense under the reasonableness test): a special expense is not payable merely because a parent requests it. The court examines necessity, the child's best interests, the family's means, and the pre separation spending pattern. A is the strongest wrong option because the child's enjoyment does not establish necessity or reasonableness. The practical consequence is that the requesting parent should show why the activity or treatment is appropriate and affordable."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A Quebec court must decide whether it has jurisdiction to hear a divorce application under the federal Divorce Act. Which statement correctly states the ordinary residence requirement in section 3 when a divorce proceeding begins in a contested court proceeding?", "options": {"A": "The spouses must both live in Quebec on the day judgment is rendered", "B": "Either spouse must have been habitually resident in Quebec for one year", "C": "The marriage must have been celebrated in the province of Quebec", "D": "The spouses must have owned a family residence in Quebec for two years"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Either spouse must have been habitually resident in Quebec for one year): section 3 of the Divorce Act gives a provincial court divorce jurisdiction when either spouse meets that residence condition. A fails because both spouses need not reside in the province at judgment. C is the strongest wrong option because the place of marriage does not replace habitual residence. The practical consequence is that residency dates must be verified before a divorce application is filed."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A parenting case includes credible evidence of coercive control, threats, and a child's fear after witnessing violent conduct. One parent argues that only physical injuries should affect the parenting decision. How should the court treat the violence evidence under section 16?", "options": {"A": "The court may ignore the evidence because no criminal charge was proved, under disputed rights analysis.", "B": "The court should consider violence only if a child was physically injured", "C": "The court must assess the violence and its impact on safety and well being", "D": "The court must deny all parenting time to the parent who was threatened"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court must assess the violence and its impact on safety and well being): section 16 requires a detailed consideration of family violence and its effect on the child and caregivers. A fails because conduct need not have produced a criminal conviction to be relevant. B is the strongest wrong option because family violence includes psychological and coercive control. The practical consequence is that the safety evidence must shape the parenting schedule and any protective conditions."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "divorce_and_family_support", "question": "A grandparent has an established relationship with a child and is not a spouse in the divorce proceeding. The grandparent wants structured visits and regular video calls with the child. What form of order can address that relationship under section 16.5?", "options": {"A": "A contact order may provide visits or communication with the child", "B": "A support order may require the parties to arrange family visits", "C": "A parenting order must transfer decision making to the grandparent", "D": "No federal order can address contact by a grandparent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A contact order may provide visits or communication with the child): section 16.5 permits contact in person or by other means of communication. B fails because a support order concerns finances. C is the strongest wrong option because contact does not require transferring decision making responsibility. The practical consequence is that the court can protect a significant relationship while preserving the child's primary parenting arrangements."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "successions", "question": "Two relatives die in the same accident, and the evidence cannot show which one survived the other. Each was named to inherit from the other under the applicable provisions. How should their successions be treated under article 616 in this situation?", "options": {"A": "The younger person is deemed to survive and inherit the older estate", "B": "The estate of the first body discovered controls both successions", "C": "They are deemed to have died at the same time for succession purposes", "D": "Both estates remain unopened until a court determines survival"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They are deemed to have died at the same time for succession purposes): article 616 applies when survival cannot be determined and one person is called to inherit from the other. A fails because age does not create a survivor. B is the strongest wrong option because the discovery order of the bodies is not the legal test. The practical consequence is that each succession passes to those who would take in the deceased person's place."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "successions", "question": "One child of the deceased died earlier and left three children, while another child of the deceased is still alive. The grandchildren claim a share of the estate by representation. How should their branch share be calculated under article 665?", "options": {"A": "The estate is divided by roots, with the grandchildren sharing one root", "B": "Each grandchild receives exactly the same share as the surviving child", "C": "The surviving child receives nothing because representation occurred", "D": "The grandchildren must divide their branch share by seniority of age"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The estate is divided by roots, with the grandchildren sharing one root): article 665 divides representation shares by roots and then among the members of each branch. B fails because each grandchild does not receive a full child's share. C is the strongest wrong option because the living child retains a root. The practical consequence is that the three grandchildren share the one share that their deceased parent would have received."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "successions", "question": "A person convicted of attempting to kill the deceased now claims a share of the estate under an otherwise valid will. Another beneficiary objects and asks whether a separate court declaration of unworthiness is needed. What does article 620 provide?", "options": {"A": "The beneficiary may inherit because the will names them expressly", "B": "The conviction makes the beneficiary unworthy of inheriting by law", "C": "Unworthiness requires a new criminal trial after the death", "D": "Only the liquidator may decide questions involving unworthiness, under the relevant contractual setting."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The conviction makes the beneficiary unworthy of inheriting by law): article 620 treats a conviction for an attempt on the deceased's life as legal unworthiness. A fails because a testamentary gift does not remove that statutory consequence. C is the strongest wrong option because the Code does not require a second criminal trial. The practical consequence is that the beneficiary cannot take the succession benefit unless another legal rule changes the result."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "successions", "question": "A will made in the presence of witnesses omits a minor formality, but the document contains the essential elements and clearly expresses the deceased's final wishes. A beneficiary asks whether the entire will must fail under article 714. What rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The will is invalid because every prescribed formality is essential, under the relevant family context.", "B": "The will may remain valid if it meets the essential requirements and clear wishes", "C": "The omitted formality may be corrected by any interested beneficiary", "D": "The will becomes valid only after every heir consents in writing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The will may remain valid if it meets the essential requirements and clear wishes): article 714 allows substantial compliance for a holograph or witnessed will. A fails because not every minor formality automatically destroys the will. C is the strongest wrong option because beneficiaries cannot amend formalities by agreement. The practical consequence is that the court examines the essential proof and whether the final wishes are clear."}, {"id": 481, "domain": "successions", "question": "Beatrice accepts her father's succession after a complete inventory shows debts exceeding the assets. She has not hidden property, mixed assets, or bypassed liquidation. A creditor now demands payment from Beatrice's personal savings under the Civil Code. What is her basic liability?", "options": {"A": "She owes every debt because acceptance creates full personal liability", "B": "She owes nothing because inheritance debts disappear at death", "C": "She owes the debts only if the liquidator names her personally", "D": "Her liability is generally limited to the value of property received"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Her liability is generally limited to the value of property received): an heir's ordinary liability for estate obligations is limited to the value of the property collected. A is the strongest wrong option because acceptance alone does not create unlimited personal liability. C adds a personal designation that ordinary heir status does not require. The practical consequence is that Beatrice's personal savings are generally protected once the estate has been properly liquidated."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "successions", "question": "After the father dies, his daughter finds an unopened envelope marked testament in his desk. The liquidator plans to ignore it because a registered notarial will already clearly exists. What legal duty should guide the liquidator before distributing estate property?", "options": {"A": "The liquidator may ignore every private document after a notarial will", "B": "The liquidator must investigate and verify a possible testamentary document", "C": "The daughter must obtain a court appointment before opening it", "D": "The envelope belongs to the heirs and may be divided as property"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The liquidator must investigate and verify a possible testamentary document): the liquidator must search for a will, have it verified when required, and take steps to execute the deceased's testamentary provisions. A fails because a known notarial will does not eliminate later or revoking documents. D is the strongest wrong option because a possible will is not ordinary property to divide. The practical consequence is that the liquidator should preserve and investigate the envelope before further distribution."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "successions", "question": "Estate debts exhaust most of the available cash, and the will contains several particular gifts without preference clauses or identified property. One beneficiary demands full payment before ordinary creditors receive their proportional shares. What is the proper result under succession law?", "options": {"A": "Particular legacies may be reduced after debts when assets are insufficient", "B": "Every particular legacy must be paid before ordinary creditors", "C": "The beneficiary may choose any estate asset to satisfy the gift", "D": "All particular legacies become the personal debts of the heirs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Particular legacies may be reduced after debts when assets are insufficient): the liquidator must reduce particular legacies when remaining assets cannot satisfy them after estate debts. B fails because unpaid estate creditors are not deferred to every particular gift. D is the strongest wrong option because unpaid particular legacies do not automatically become personal heir debts. The practical consequence is that the beneficiary may receive less than the stated gift according to the statutory reduction order."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "successions", "question": "The estate has several valuable assets and only a modest known debt. Before the liquidator completes payment and accounts, two heirs want to sign a deed dividing the house immediately. What legal obstacle applies to their proposed partition at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Partition cannot occur before liquidation has been completed", "B": "Partition requires every beneficiary to surrender the property", "C": "Partition may occur only after the ten-year option period", "D": "Partition is unavailable when the estate includes a house"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Partition cannot occur before liquidation has been completed): the estate must be liquidated before the heirs proceed to partition. B fails because no beneficiary must surrender property as a condition of partition. D is the strongest wrong option because the presence of a house does not make partition legally impossible. The practical consequence is that the heirs must wait for payment and the final liquidation account before dividing the house."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "successions", "question": "Martin dies intestate with a spouse and three children. After valid debts and liquidation costs are paid, the distributive estate is worth three hundred sixty thousand dollars. What share does each child receive under the intestacy rules of the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "Each child receives one sixth of the estate", "B": "Each child receives one third of the estate", "C": "Each child receives one ninth of the estate", "D": "Each child receives one quarter of the estate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Each child receives one sixth of the estate): with a spouse and descendants, the spouse takes one third and the descendants share the remaining two thirds. B is the strongest wrong option because one third is the collective descendant share, not each child's share. D ignores the surviving spouse's statutory share. The practical consequence is that each of the three children receives one sixth of the distributive estate."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "successions", "question": "A court declares a missing man dead after seven years without contact. The judgment identifies the presumed date of death, and his niece now asks when the succession opens. What legal effect does the declaration of death produce before probate proceeds?", "options": {"A": "The succession opens only when the missing man's body is recovered", "B": "The succession opens when the niece applies for a death certificate", "C": "The judgment produces the same effect as an actual death", "D": "The succession remains suspended until the missing man reaches eighty"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The judgment produces the same effect as an actual death): a declaratory judgment of death produces the legal effects of death. A fails because the declaration does not depend on recovery of the body. B is the strongest wrong option because a certificate application is administrative evidence rather than the event that opens the succession. The practical consequence is that the estate is administered according to the date fixed by the judgment."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "successions", "question": "During probate, evidence shows that a nephew concealed his uncle's later will and pressured the notary to suppress it. The deceased never forgave him, and another successor asks the court to act. What may the court declare about the nephew's right to inherit?", "options": {"A": "The nephew must receive a reduced share of the estate", "B": "The nephew may be declared unworthy based on that conduct", "C": "The nephew inherits until a criminal conviction is obtained", "D": "The nephew's misconduct affects only the liquidator's fees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The nephew may be declared unworthy based on that conduct): a court may declare unworthiness for concealing or destroying a will or interfering with testamentary freedom. A fails because the remedy is not an automatic fractional reduction. C is the strongest wrong option because this declared unworthiness does not require a criminal conviction. The practical consequence is that the nephew may lose the inheritance if the court grants the request."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "successions", "question": "A spouse learns after the death that the marriage was invalid because of a hidden prior union. She acted honestly throughout the relationship and had no notice of the impediment. Can she claim a surviving spouse's rights under Quebec law?", "options": {"A": "No, because a null marriage prevents every marital right", "B": "Yes, but only if the deceased confirmed the marriage in a will", "C": "No, because nullity applies retroactively against both spouses, under the current factual circumstances.", "D": "The good-faith spouse may inherit despite the post-death nullity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The good-faith spouse may inherit despite the post-death nullity): a spouse who acted in good faith may inherit when nullity of the marriage is pronounced after the death. A fails because the protection exists precisely for certain cases of nullity. B is the strongest wrong option because a testamentary confirmation is not the statutory condition. The practical consequence is that the spouse's good faith and knowledge at the relevant time must be examined."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "successions", "question": "Maria dies intestate without children. Her spouse and both parents survive, and her two brothers also ask about their rights. After a proper liquidation, how should Maria's distributive estate be divided under the statutory order before the liquidator distributes the residue?", "options": {"A": "The spouse takes one half while the parents share one half", "B": "The spouse takes one third while the parents share two thirds", "C": "The spouse takes two thirds while the parents share one third", "D": "The spouse and the two brothers divide the estate equally, under the current Quebec analysis."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The spouse takes two thirds while the parents share one third): without descendants, the surviving spouse receives two thirds and the privileged ascendants share the remaining third. A fails because an equal split does not match this statutory order. B is the strongest wrong option because it reverses the spouse's preferential share. The practical consequence is that each parent receives one sixth while the spouse receives two thirds."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "successions", "question": "Arthur's witnessed will was signed by two people he trusted, but one witness was only sixteen. The second witness was an adult and all signatures otherwise satisfy the form. What effect does the minor witness create on the signed document under Quebec law?", "options": {"A": "The will fails because a witness to this form must be an adult", "B": "The will remains valid because one adult witness was present", "C": "The will remains valid if the minor understood the document", "D": "The will becomes holograph when the minor witness is excluded"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The will fails because a witness to this form must be an adult): a witness to a will before witnesses must be a major. B fails because two qualified witnesses are required rather than one. C is the strongest wrong option because a minor's understanding does not make the minor a qualified witness. The practical consequence is that the witnessed form is not valid without a second qualified witness."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "successions", "question": "Yves' will says that the estate must give a painting owned by a friend to Yves' niece. The will shows no intention to purchase or substitute that painting. What is the effect of the gift when the estate is administered?", "options": {"A": "The gift of another person's property is ineffective in these facts", "B": "The liquidator must buy the painting from the friend, under the current family context.", "C": "The niece receives a cash amount equal to the painting's value", "D": "The friend must transfer the painting because Yves requested it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The gift of another person's property is ineffective in these facts): a legacy of property owned by another person is ineffective unless the will shows an intention to oblige the heir to procure it. B fails because no purchase obligation appears in the facts. C is the strongest wrong option because a cash substitute would require a contrary intention. The practical consequence is that the friend keeps the painting unless the friend independently agrees to transfer it."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "successions", "question": "A minor under representation inherits a substantial estate. No authorized person renounces before the applicable option period expires, and the representative later asks whether silence accepted the succession. How should the inheritance be treated now before the estate is distributed?", "options": {"A": "The minor receives nothing until reaching full legal age", "B": "The succession is deemed accepted unless the representative validly renounces", "C": "The succession remains undecided until the minor reaches majority", "D": "The succession is deemed renounced because silence protects the minor"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The succession is deemed accepted unless the representative validly renounces): a succession devolved to a represented minor is deemed accepted when no valid renunciation occurs within the option period. A fails because the minor can hold rights through representation. C is the strongest wrong option because the decision is not postponed automatically to majority. The practical consequence is that the representative must act with the required authorization if renunciation is intended."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "successions", "question": "A liquidator discovers fresh produce and a commercial freezer filled with fish while waiting for the inventory to be completed. Storage costs are high and the market may collapse within days. What may the liquidator do with these assets under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The liquidator should preserve the food until every creditor is identified", "B": "The liquidator should distribute the food among the legal successors", "C": "The liquidator should donate the food and record the market value", "D": "The liquidator may sell property that is perishable or costly to preserve"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The liquidator may sell property that is perishable or costly to preserve): a liquidator may dispose of perishable, rapidly depreciating, or expensive-to-keep movable property without ordinary heir consent. A fails because waiting may destroy value and increase costs. B is the strongest wrong option because liquidation does not permit early distribution to the heirs. The practical consequence is that the estate may sell the food promptly and preserve the proceeds for creditors and beneficiaries."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "successions", "question": "A wealthy parent leaves the entire estate to a registered charity. One independent adult child has ample means and no support claim. The child argues that descendants must receive a fixed inheritance share. What result follows under Quebec law now?", "options": {"A": "The child receives one half because descendants have a forced share", "B": "The testamentary gift is valid because Quebec has no fixed forced share", "C": "The child receives the estate unless the charity files a court claim", "D": "The will is invalid because it excludes every legal successor"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The testamentary gift is valid because Quebec has no fixed forced share): Quebec protects certain family and support rights, but it does not reserve every child a fixed fraction of the estate. A fails because no general one-half forced share exists. C is the strongest wrong option because the charity does not bear an unexplained burden to defend the will. The practical consequence is that an independent child without a support claim cannot override the charitable gift."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "property", "question": "A landowner wants to excavate minerals beneath her land without a permit because her deed grants surface ownership. The property contains resources affected by public rights. What limit legally applies to her ownership beneath the surface under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The landowner controls minerals whenever the deed grants surface rights", "B": "The municipality owns all minerals beneath private land", "C": "Subsurface rights belong to the owner of the ground floor", "D": "Public rights over minerals qualify the owner's rights beneath the surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Public rights over minerals qualify the owner's rights beneath the surface): surface ownership includes the subsurface in principle, subject to statutory public rights over minerals and other resources. A fails because a deed does not erase those public limits. B is the strongest wrong option because municipalities do not automatically own all subsurface minerals. The practical consequence is that the owner must satisfy the applicable resource-law requirements before excavation."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "property", "question": "A buyer purchases a used canoe from a person who is not its owner. The buyer acts in good faith at the transaction and keeps the canoe for three years. The true owner then discovers its location. What consequence may follow after that period?", "options": {"A": "The true owner recovers movable property for up to ten years", "B": "A good-faith movable possessor may acquire ownership after three years", "C": "The buyer acquires no ownership without an original purchase receipt", "D": "The canoe automatically returns to its first seller, under the client's evidence record."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A good-faith movable possessor may acquire ownership after three years): a good-faith possessor of a movable acquires it after three years from the owner's dispossession, subject to the law. A fails because the ten-year period concerns immovables rather than this canoe. C is the strongest wrong option because the original receipt is not the source of the prescription rule. The practical consequence is that the true owner's claim may be extinguished after the three-year period."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "property", "question": "A good-faith possessor makes useful repairs to an estate building, and those improvements still exist when the true owner recovers the property. The owner offers either reimbursement or the increase in market value. What obligation governs useful expenses under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The possessor receives nothing because the owner's title controls", "B": "The possessor may keep the building until it becomes worthless", "C": "The owner must compensate useful expenses if improvements still exist", "D": "The possessor must buy the land at its current market value"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The owner must compensate useful expenses if improvements still exist): a good-faith possessor is entitled to reimbursement or the added value for useful improvements that remain. A fails because registered title does not eliminate the possessor's expense protection. B is the strongest wrong option because retention is limited to securing eligible reimbursement. The practical consequence is that the owner chooses between reimbursing the expense and paying the resulting plus-value."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "property", "question": "A sculptor transforms another person's raw stone into a finished artwork worth several times the original material. The stone owner demands the artwork while the sculptor seeks ownership. What rule normally decides who acquires the transformed movable under accession rules?", "options": {"A": "The original material owner owns the transformed product in this case", "B": "The sculptor acquires the new thing when work exceeds material value", "C": "The buyer of the artwork becomes its first legal owner", "D": "The sculptor and material owner must sell the artwork immediately"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The sculptor acquires the new thing when work exceeds material value): transformation can transfer ownership when the value of the work exceeds the value of the material. A fails because material ownership does not always control the transformed result. C is the strongest wrong option because a later buyer cannot become the first owner through accession. The practical consequence is that the stone owner receives compensation while the sculptor keeps the artwork."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "property", "question": "A homeowner complains about ordinary daytime construction noise, occasional dust, and brief access by workers from a neighbouring renovation. The disturbance follows local bylaws and reasonable practices. Must the homeowner reasonably tolerate these ordinary effects under current Quebec neighbour law?", "options": {"A": "The homeowner may stop every construction effect through an injunction", "B": "Any dust crossing a boundary creates strict liability, under the client's property setting.", "C": "The workers need consent from every neighbour before entering a street", "D": "Normal neighbourhood inconvenience within local tolerance must be accepted"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Normal neighbourhood inconvenience within local tolerance must be accepted): neighbours must tolerate ordinary effects that do not exceed reasonable local tolerance. A fails because ordinary construction effects do not justify stopping every activity. B is the strongest wrong option because brief lawful dust does not create strict liability. The practical consequence is that the homeowner cannot recover for effects that remain normal and reasonable."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "property", "question": "An uphill owner redirects a natural spring and channels large volumes of construction runoff toward a lower neighbour. The new flow floods the neighbour's basement and erodes the yard. What limit applies to changes in water flow under neighbour law?", "options": {"A": "The uphill owner may redirect all water after improving the property", "B": "The lower owner must accept any increased flow from development", "C": "The municipality bears responsibility for every private drainage change", "D": "The upper owner cannot aggravate the natural flow onto lower land"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The upper owner cannot aggravate the natural flow onto lower land): an upper owner may manage natural drainage but cannot intensify the burden on the lower property. A fails because improving the upper property does not authorize flooding a neighbour. B is the strongest wrong option because development does not create permission to increase runoff substantially. The practical consequence is that the lower owner may claim remediation for the artificial flooding and erosion."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "property", "question": "Three people own a building in indivision and disagree about repairing a leaking common roof. Two owners approve the ordinary repair, while the third refuses. The roof will worsen if nothing is done this week. What voting rule governs this administration decision?", "options": {"A": "Ordinary administration decisions require a majority in number and shares", "B": "Every routine repair needs unanimous consent from all co-owners", "C": "The co-owner with the largest single share decides alone", "D": "The municipality chooses the contractor when owners disagree"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ordinary administration decisions require a majority in number and shares): routine management of indivision is decided by a majority measured both in people and in ownership shares. B fails because unanimity is reserved for major acts. C is the strongest wrong option because the largest holder does not receive unilateral management power. The practical consequence is that the two approving owners must also represent a majority of the shares."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "property", "question": "A mother gives her daughter the right to use and rent an apartment building for life, but the daughter must preserve the building's substance. The mother keeps bare ownership and cannot occupy the property. What right does the daughter hold?", "options": {"A": "She holds a servitude because the building serves her income", "B": "The daughter holds usufruct and must preserve the property's substance", "C": "She holds full ownership because she collects the rent", "D": "She holds a personal lease terminable at the mother's choice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The daughter holds usufruct and must preserve the property's substance): usufruct gives use and enjoyment of another person's property for a limited time while preserving its substance. A fails because a servitude burdens property for another property's benefit. C is the strongest wrong option because receiving rent does not transfer bare ownership. The practical consequence is that the daughter keeps income while the mother retains ownership subject to the usufruct."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "property", "question": "A dominant lot benefiting from a drainage servitude is sold to a new owner. The owner of the serving lot argues that the sale ended the original owner's personal arrangement. Does the new owner continue to benefit from the servitude?", "options": {"A": "The servitude ends automatically when the dominant lot is sold", "B": "A servitude follows the dominant and serving land after ownership changes", "C": "The new owner must negotiate a fresh agreement with the serving owner", "D": "Only the original owner's family may continue using the drainage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A servitude follows the dominant and serving land after ownership changes): the right attaches to the properties and survives the sale of either one. A fails because transfer of the dominant lot does not extinguish the servitude. C is the strongest wrong option because the purchaser inherits the existing land relationship rather than needing a new contract. The practical consequence is that the new dominant owner may enforce the registered right subject to publication rules."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "property", "question": "A worker digging on the landowner's property finds buried gold coins while performing work for the landowner. Nobody can establish another owner of the treasure, and the discovery occurs within the landowner's land. How is the treasure legally divided under the Code?", "options": {"A": "The worker receives the entire treasure because he found it while digging", "B": "The State receives the treasure because buried gold belongs to the public domain", "C": "The treasure belongs to the landowner because the discovery was made for the owner", "D": "The worker and landowner divide every coin immediately in equal shares"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The treasure belongs to the landowner because the discovery was made for the owner): treasure found in a person's land belongs to that landowner when the finder acted for the owner. A fails because the worker discovered the coins in the course of work for the landowner. B is the strongest wrong option because buried treasure is not automatically public property. The practical consequence is that the landowner owns the coins subject to any separate contractual claim."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "property", "question": "A divided co-ownership governs a residential building. Co-owners representing eighty-five percent of all co-owners and ninety-five percent of votes want to terminate the divided co-ownership and sell the whole building at once. The remaining co-owners oppose the plan and ask which voting threshold governs termination.", "options": {"A": "A simple majority of co-owners is enough to terminate", "B": "One owner with more than fifty percent of votes decides alone", "C": "Termination requires unanimous written consent from every co-owner and creditor", "D": "Ending co-ownership requires three quarters of co-owners holding ninety percent of votes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ending co-ownership requires three quarters of co-owners holding ninety percent of votes): article 1108 requires a majority of three quarters of all co-owners representing ninety percent of the votes. The stated support exceeds both thresholds, so termination can proceed if the other formal requirements are met; opposition alone does not defeat a valid decision."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "property", "question": "A property owner promises a neighbour in a private writing not to follow a mandatory environmental rule about waste storage. The owner later relies on the promise when a regulator intervenes and asks whether the agreement prevents enforcement. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "Private agreements cannot waive mandatory environmental limits on ownership", "B": "The promise binds the regulator because both owners agreed to it", "C": "The environmental rule applies only if registered against the land", "D": "The neighbour becomes responsible for the owner's waste storage, under the written consumer context."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Private agreements cannot waive mandatory environmental limits on ownership): property rights remain subject to mandatory law and public order. B fails because a private promise cannot prevent a regulator from enforcing a statute. C is the strongest wrong option because registration is not required for a mandatory public rule. The practical consequence is that the environmental enforcement continues despite the owners' agreement."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "property", "question": "A donor gives land with a temporary written prohibition on sale intended to preserve a family orchard. Ten years later the orchard no longer exists, and the recipient urgently needs to sell part of the land for medical expenses. May a court allow the transfer?", "options": {"A": "A temporary inalienability clause cannot be lifted early", "B": "Yes, but only if the donor's original notary purchases the land", "C": "A court may authorize disposition when the justified protective interest has disappeared", "D": "Yes, automatically after five years under the clause, under the procedural legal framework."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A court may authorize disposition when the justified protective interest has disappeared): a temporary inalienability clause may be lifted when the interest supporting it no longer exists or a stronger interest requires sale. A fails because the restriction is not absolute. B is the strongest wrong option because the donor's notary receives no special purchase right. The practical consequence is that the court may permit the necessary transfer and impose protective conditions."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "property", "question": "Before subdividing one large property, the owner signs a written declaration that establishes a permanent drainage path over one part for the benefit of other future lots. After the subdivision, a buyer challenges the drainage right. What source may support the servitude?", "options": {"A": "A servitude cannot be established before separate owners exist", "B": "Only a later court judgment can create the drainage arrangement", "C": "The presence of drains automatically creates a public servitude", "D": "A written subdivision declaration may establish a servitude by destination"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A written subdivision declaration may establish a servitude by destination): a servitude by destination of the owner is established in a writing that plans future division and fixes the right's nature and location. A fails because separate ownership need not exist at the moment of declaration. B is the strongest wrong option because no later judgment is required to create the source. The practical consequence is that the drainage right may bind the resulting lots when the plan is implemented."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "property", "question": "A serving farm burdened by a drainage servitude is divided among several new owners. The dominant owner fears that the division will allow each new farm owner to block or reduce the drainage. What effect does division of the serving land have on the servitude?", "options": {"A": "The division does not impair the dominant owner's existing right", "B": "The servitude ends because one serving owner now controls only part", "C": "Every new lot receives a separate right to increase the drainage flow", "D": "The dominant owner must purchase each divided serving lot"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The division does not impair the dominant owner's existing right): dividing the serving land does not destroy the servitude or enlarge the dominant owner's obligations. B fails because each new serving owner remains subject to the existing burden. C is the strongest wrong option because the dominant owner cannot increase the flow merely because the land was divided. The practical consequence is that the servitude continues against the divided lots without a new contract."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "property", "question": "A servitude title permits passage by foot three times each day, but a court later considers how the mode of exercise is established. The dominant owner claims a wider modern use while the serving owner relies on the original terms. What principle governs the scope of exercise?", "options": {"A": "The dominant owner may modernize the route in any manner", "B": "The serving owner may change the route annually without notice", "C": "The title or its prescription may fix the mode of exercise", "D": "The original written terms have no effect after five years"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The title or its prescription may fix the mode of exercise): the title and subsequent legal development define how the servitude may be used. A fails because the dominant owner cannot expand the burden freely. B is the strongest wrong option because the serving owner cannot alter the route at will. The practical consequence is that the disputed passage must be measured against the established mode of exercise."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "property", "question": "A landowner transfers ownership of a future building above the land while retaining ownership of the ground. The deed does not state that the surface right must end on a particular fixed date. What maximum duration may this superficies have?", "options": {"A": "It must end after thirty years like a conventional indivision", "B": "It may be perpetual because superficies does not require a fixed term", "C": "It lasts only for ten years unless renewed by a notary", "D": "It expires when the first constructed building is sold, under the procedural civil-law context."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It may be perpetual because superficies does not require a fixed term): superficies can be perpetual unless the constituting agreement sets an end date or condition. A fails because thirty years is not the maximum for this right. C is the strongest wrong option because no general ten-year limit applies. The practical consequence is that the surface ownership continues perpetually until a valid ending event occurs."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "property", "question": "A lender takes possession of a valuable watch as security for a loan and agrees to return it when the debt is paid. The borrower later asks whether the transaction creates a hypothec even though the debtor no longer possesses the watch. What kind of security has been created?", "options": {"A": "A legal servitude imposed over the borrowed watch, under the procedural factual circumstances.", "B": "An emphytéose granting long-term use of movable property", "C": "An unsecured personal claim with no property right", "D": "A movable hypothec with dispossession, also called a pledge"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A movable hypothec with dispossession, also called a pledge): a lender holding movable collateral as security has a hypothec with dispossession. A fails because a servitude burdens an immovable for another property's benefit. B is the strongest wrong option because emphyteusis concerns long-term use of an immovable. The practical consequence is that the lender has a real security right in the watch until the secured debt is paid."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A book club sends an unsolicited novel and states that the recipient's silence will create a binding purchase after fourteen days. The recipient never ordered a book, never opened the parcel, and ignores the notice. Was a contract formed through silence?", "options": {"A": "Silence creates acceptance whenever a merchant gives written notice", "B": "The recipient accepted by keeping the unopened parcel at home", "C": "A contract formed when fourteen days expired automatically", "D": "Silence does not normally constitute acceptance without a legal or factual basis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Silence does not normally constitute acceptance without a legal or factual basis): a contract requires communicated consent, and passive silence is generally insufficient. A fails because a merchant's unilateral notice cannot override that rule. B is the strongest wrong option because keeping an unordered parcel does not communicate an intention to buy. The practical consequence is that the recipient has not purchased the novel."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A farmer agrees in writing to sell the entire harvest from a specifically identified field during the coming season. The contract provides a formula that makes quantity and price determinable from the field's actual yield. Is the object sufficiently certain for a valid obligation?", "options": {"A": "Future property cannot be the object of a contractual obligation, under the procedural evidence record.", "B": "The object is invalid unless the exact kilograms are fixed today", "C": "The agreement remains an offer until the buyer inspects every plant", "D": "A future object is valid when it is determinate or can be determined"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A future object is valid when it is determinate or can be determined): contractual object need not exist at signing if the agreement supplies a reliable method of identification. A fails because future property may validly be the subject of obligations. B is the strongest wrong option because it requires present numerical certainty instead of determinability. The practical consequence is that the harvest contract may be enforced once the field's yield is measured."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "obligations", "question": "An insurer pays its insured for fire damage caused by a contractor's fault and then sues the contractor to recover the amount paid. The contractor argues the insurer never had a contract with him and cannot pursue the claim. What principle allows the insurer to act?", "options": {"A": "The insurer becomes owner of every asset held by the contractor", "B": "Legal subrogation lets the insurer exercise the insured's rights against the liable party", "C": "The insured must assign the claim through a new public auction", "D": "The contractor's fault automatically becomes a public offence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Legal subrogation lets the insurer exercise the insured's rights against the liable party): after indemnifying the insured, the insurer may step into the insured's claim against the person responsible. A fails because subrogation does not transfer the contractor's entire patrimony. C is the strongest wrong option because legal subrogation does not require a new public auction. The practical consequence is that the insurer may recover the covered amount from the contractor within the insured's rights."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A standard-form equipment lease contains an ambiguous maintenance term. The supplier drafted every clause and the customer had no realistic chance to negotiate changes. If the clause reasonably supports two meanings, which interpretation should prevail under the current Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The supplier's preferred meaning controls because it prepared the contract", "B": "The first dictionary definition generally governs an ambiguous lease term", "C": "The reasonable interpretation favouring the adhering customer should prevail", "D": "The clause becomes legally null because the parties dispute its meaning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The reasonable interpretation favouring the adhering customer should prevail): article 1432 resolves doubt against the person who stipulated the obligation and in favour of the adhering party or consumer. A fails because drafting the document does not entitle the supplier to the more favourable meaning. B is the strongest wrong option because interpretation considers context, not a mechanical dictionary rule. The consequence is that the customer receives the reasonable reading that favours that adhering party."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A service agreement contains fifteen independent clauses. One clause unlawfully authorizes the provider to retain the customer's identity documents indefinitely. The remaining obligations can operate without that clause. What happens to the agreement under the Civil Code's severability rule for contracts?", "options": {"A": "The entire agreement becomes null because one clause is unlawful, under the private civil-law context.", "B": "The unlawful clause is null while the remaining divisible agreement continues", "C": "The court must rewrite the unlawful clause into a reasonable retention period", "D": "The customer may choose either the entire agreement or its unlawful clause"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The unlawful clause is null while the remaining divisible agreement continues): article 1438 limits nullity to the invalid clause unless the contract can exist only as an indivisible whole. A fails because one unlawful clause does not automatically destroy fifteen independent obligations. C is the strongest wrong option because the court applies the agreement rather than drafting replacement terms. The consequence is that the identity-retention clause falls away while the other services remain governed."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A mechanic lawfully possesses a customer's car and has completed repairs. The repair bill is due, and the unpaid claim is closely connected to that vehicle. The customer demands return without paying. May the mechanic legally retain the car temporarily?", "options": {"A": "No, a repairer must surrender the vehicle whenever the owner demands it", "B": "Yes, because every unpaid claim gives the mechanic possession of any property", "C": "Yes, when the exigible debt is closely connected to the vehicle held with consent", "D": "Yes, because a repairer receives ownership until the repair bill is paid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, when the exigible debt is closely connected to the vehicle held with consent): article 1592 gives a creditor a right to retain specific property when an exigible connected claim remains unpaid. A fails because lawful possession and the connection support retention. B is the strongest wrong option because retention does not extend to unrelated property. The consequence is that the mechanic may hold the car temporarily pending payment of the connected bill."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A private creditor telephones its debtor and demands immediate delivery of promised equipment. The contract contains no automatic default clause, and no urgent circumstance applies. The debtor asks whether the call validly put it in default in these circumstances under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The telephone demand validly created default because the creditor clearly requested performance", "B": "The demand was ineffective because extrajudicial default requires a writing and reasonable time", "C": "The demand was ineffective because only a court may create contractual default, under private Quebec analysis.", "D": "The demand validly created default because equipment delivery requires no formal notice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The demand was ineffective because extrajudicial default requires a writing and reasonable time): article 1595 requires a written extrajudicial demand and sufficient time for performance. A fails because the creditor's clear oral request does not satisfy the writing requirement. C is the strongest wrong option because a creditor may put the debtor in default by extrajudicial demand or judicial application. The consequence is that the debtor was not validly placed in default by the telephone call."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "obligations", "question": "Without the debtor being otherwise in default, a creditor files a judicial application for performance. The debtor then performs within a reasonable time after receiving the application. Who bears the costs of that judicial demand under the current Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "The debtor generally bears the costs because filing a judicial demand creates default", "B": "Each party bears half because performance occurred after the application was filed", "C": "The court allocates costs without considering whether the debtor performed promptly", "D": "The creditor bears the costs because the debtor performed within a reasonable time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The creditor bears the costs because the debtor performed within a reasonable time): article 1596 gives a debtor who was not otherwise in default a reasonable time to perform after a judicial application. A fails because filing the application does not erase the statutory protection. B is the strongest wrong option because the rule does not prescribe an equal cost split. The consequence is that the creditor cannot recover the costs caused by its premature demand."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A caterer promises to deliver meals for a wedding beginning that evening. By late afternoon the caterer has not prepared or delivered anything, and there is no time to cure. Must the organizer first send a written formal demand before relying on default?", "options": {"A": "Yes, a written formal demand must precede every remedy for missed performance", "B": "Yes, the organizer must wait until the wedding ends before claiming default", "C": "No, the missed evening event puts the caterer automatically in default by operation of law", "D": "No, the caterer remains outside default until the organizer proves financial injury"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, the missed evening event puts the caterer automatically in default by operation of law): article 1597 creates automatic default where performance is useful only within an expiring time or urgent performance was required immediately. A fails because not every default requires an extrajudicial writing. B is the strongest wrong option because default arises when timely usefulness is lost, not after the event ends. The consequence is that the organizer may rely on default without a prior formal demand."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "obligations", "question": "After a garage fails to complete promised repairs, the owner sends a written default demand that expressly authorizes another garage to perform the work at the first garage's expense. The repairs are completed. What may the owner claim from the defaulting garage?", "options": {"A": "The owner may recover the reasonable substitute repair cost from the first garage", "B": "The owner may recover the full original repair price as a punitive penalty", "C": "The owner must pay both garages and may not claim reimbursement, under the private evidence record.", "D": "The owner may claim only nominal damages because repairs are now complete"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The owner may recover the reasonable substitute repair cost from the first garage): article 1602 allows a creditor to have the obligation performed at the debtor's expense after default and proper notice. B fails because a substitute repair cost is compensatory rather than punitive. C is the strongest wrong option because the defaulting garage must bear the reasonable replacement cost. The consequence is that the owner may claim the amount reasonably paid to the substitute garage."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A tenant contracts not to alter the building's facade. Without permission, the tenant installs a large permanent sign that violates that duty. The landlord asks the court to authorize removal at the tenant's cost. Is that remedy available under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "No, because a landlord may only recover damages for breach of a promise not to alter", "B": "No, because the tenant's installation became part of the leased property immediately", "C": "Yes, the court may authorize removal at the tenant's expense after the prohibited alteration", "D": "Yes, the landlord may remove the sign personally and also recover punitive damages"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, the court may authorize removal at the tenant's expense after the prohibited alteration): article 1603 permits destruction or removal at the debtor's expense where the debtor violated an obligation not to do something. A fails because damages are not the only consequence of such a breach. B is the strongest wrong option because attachment to the building does not neutralize the negative obligation. The consequence is that the landlord may seek judicial authorization to remove the permanent sign."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "obligations", "question": "After a supplier's breach, the buyer receives an insurance payment covering part of the same loss. The insurer has not been subrogated to the buyer's claim against the supplier. Can the supplier reduce contractual damages by the insurance amount under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "Yes, the supplier may reduce damages by every third-party benefit the buyer receives", "B": "No, the third-party payment does not reduce the claim unless the insurer is subrogated", "C": "Yes, the buyer must repay the insurer before suing the supplier for any loss", "D": "No, but the insurer automatically becomes owner of the buyer's entire claim"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, the third-party payment does not reduce the claim unless the insurer is subrogated): article 1608 preserves the debtor's damages obligation despite a third-person benefit unless that person is subrogated. A fails because it treats every collateral payment as reducing the contractual claim. C is the strongest wrong option because the buyer need not repay the insurer before pursuing the supplier. The consequence is that the buyer may recover the damages without a reduction for this insurance payment."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A debtor transfers valuable assets to a friend for a nominal sum after learning that a judgment is imminent. The transfer leaves insufficient property to pay an existing creditor. The creditor seeks to disregard that transaction. What action fits the facts?", "options": {"A": "The creditor may use an oblique action to collect the friend's personal debt", "B": "The creditor may demand criminal prosecution before attacking the transfer, under the private succession context.", "C": "The creditor may seek a declaration that the fraudulent transfer is not opposable", "D": "The creditor may cancel the transfer only by proving the friend paid market value"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The creditor may seek a declaration that the fraudulent transfer is not opposable): article 1631 protects a creditor from a juridical act made in fraud of the creditor's rights. A fails because an oblique action targets a neglected right of the debtor, not the friend's own debt. B is the strongest wrong option because a criminal proceeding is not a precondition for this civil protection. The consequence is that the transfer may not be set up against the creditor even if it remains valid between others."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A parent contracts with a school to pay a child's tuition directly. The contract clearly identifies the child as the person intended to benefit, and the school accepts the obligation. What right does the child have under the stipulation for another?", "options": {"A": "The child has no right because only parties to the contract may claim performance", "B": "The child has only a moral expectation until the parent assigns the tuition claim", "C": "The child may demand performance only after a court appoints a tutor for the claim", "D": "The child may demand the promised performance directly from the school as beneficiary"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The child may demand the promised performance directly from the school as beneficiary): article 1444 gives the third-party beneficiary a direct right to demand performance from the promisor. A fails because the stipulation is an exception to simple privity. C is the strongest wrong option because no tutorship appointment is required for this direct contractual right. The consequence is that the child may enforce the tuition promise directly."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "obligations", "question": "An insurer pays an insured's covered loss and intends to pursue the responsible party. The insurer and insured sign a document expressly stating that the insurer is subrogated to the insured's claim. Is the conventional subrogation valid under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "Yes, conventional subrogation is valid when express and made in writing", "B": "Yes, paying any loss automatically creates conventional subrogation without a document", "C": "No, subrogation requires the responsible party's prior written consent", "D": "No, an insurer may rarely take the insured's contractual or extracontractual claim"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, conventional subrogation is valid when express and made in writing): article 1653 requires conventional subrogation to be express and in writing. B fails because payment alone does not create conventional subrogation. C is the strongest wrong option because the responsible party's consent is not required for the insurer and insured to agree. The consequence is that the insurer may step into the insured's claim subject to its existing limits and defences."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A creditor has a strong interest in receiving a confidential consulting report personally from the named debtor. An unrelated company offers to perform the same report instead. The creditor refuses the third party's offer. May the third party force the creditor to accept that performance?", "options": {"A": "No, because the creditor has an interest in personal performance by the debtor", "B": "Yes, because any competent third party may replace the named debtor", "C": "Yes, because refusing an equally valuable report causes undue delay, under the family civil-law context.", "D": "No, because third-party payment is forbidden in every contractual setting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because the creditor has an interest in personal performance by the debtor): article 1555 permits third-person payment generally but protects a creditor with a legitimate interest in performance by the debtor personally. B fails because a competent substitute is not enough when personal performance is the protected interest. D is the strongest wrong option because the Code permits third-person payment in many situations. The consequence is that the creditor may refuse the unrelated company's offer."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "obligations", "question": "After paying a loan in full, a borrower requests evidence of discharge and the original debt instrument. The lender confirms receipt of payment but refuses both requests. What is the borrower entitled to demand under the current governing Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "Only a verbal confirmation because written proof is optional", "B": "A new loan contract replacing the paid instrument automatically", "C": "An acquittance and the turning over of the original title", "D": "A court judgment before any evidence of payment must be provided"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An acquittance and the turning over of the original title): article 1568 entitles a paying debtor to an acquittance and the original title of the obligation. A fails because the right concerns documentary evidence rather than optional verbal confirmation. B is the strongest wrong option because full payment extinguishes rather than recreates the loan. The consequence is that the borrower may demand both documents from the lender."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A borrower and lender agree to replace a monthly payment schedule with a longer schedule. They do not state that the original debt is extinguished, and no new consideration or separate debt is identified. Does this change necessarily create a novation?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because any new payment schedule extinguishes the earlier obligation", "B": "No, because novation is not presumed and the required intention is not evident", "C": "Yes, because changing a payment date generally substitutes a new debt", "D": "No, because only courts may recognize a changed payment schedule"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, because novation is not presumed and the required intention is not evident): article 1661 requires the intention to substitute and extinguish an old obligation to be evident. A fails because a changed payment schedule may merely modify the existing debt. C is the strongest wrong option because changing a date does not automatically create a substituted obligation. The consequence is that the original debt remains subject to the revised schedule unless true novation is established."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A debtor tells a friend to pay the creditor. The friend then personally promises the creditor that the friend will make the payment, and the creditor accepts that personal undertaking. How should the friend's role be classified under the Civil Code?", "options": {"A": "A mere payment agent because any third-party payer remains outside the contract", "B": "A surety because every payment by a third person creates suretyship", "C": "A delegate because the friend personally undertook payment to the creditor", "D": "A new creditor because the friend promised to send funds to another person"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A delegate because the friend personally undertook payment to the creditor): article 1667 distinguishes delegation from a mere payment indication by requiring the delegate to assume a personal obligation toward the creditor. A fails because an agent-like indication does not involve a personal promise. B is the strongest wrong option because suretyship is a different relationship and is not created by every third-party payment. The consequence is that the friend is personally bound to the creditor as delegate."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A sale states that the seller bears every risk until the buyer receives the goods. Before delivery, an unforeseeable and irresistible fire destroys the goods in the seller's warehouse. The seller invokes superior force and asks to be released. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The seller is released because superior force defeats every contractual duty", "B": "The express risk allocation prevents release because the seller assumed that risk", "C": "The buyer must sue the warehouse owner because the seller lacked custody", "D": "The obligation becomes conditional until the seller obtains replacement goods, under the family contractual setting."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The express risk allocation prevents release because the seller assumed that risk): article 1693 permits release through superior force unless the debtor has expressly assumed the risk. A fails because superior force does not override a specific contractual allocation. C is the strongest wrong option because another potential defendant does not remove the seller's undertaking. The consequence is that the seller remains responsible for the destroyed goods despite the exceptional fire."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A provider files three required monthly reports one day late, causing minor inconvenience but no lasting harm. The customer invokes a single missed deadline to demand full resiliation of the year-long service. The provider has otherwise performed every obligation according to the contract. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "Repeated minor defaults support a proportionate reduction, not automatic full resiliation", "B": "One late report is enough because any missed deadline is substantial default", "C": "Repeated delay automatically entitles the customer to cancel and avoid all payments", "D": "The provider's conduct has no legal consequence because the reports were delivered"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeated minor defaults support a proportionate reduction, not automatic full resiliation): article 1604 bars full resolution or resiliation for a minor default even in a successive contract, while allowing reduction when minor breaches repeat. B fails because a one-day delay is not automatically substantial. C is the strongest wrong option because it exaggerates proportional reduction into total cancellation. The consequence is that the customer may seek a reduced obligation and proven damages while the service continues."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "obligations", "question": "A client pays a deposit to a portrait artist who promised to paint the work personally. The artist dies before starting, and the estate offers a different painter while demanding the remaining fee. The client rejects the substitute. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The estate may appoint any qualified painter because the debt is only financial", "B": "The client must accept the substitute because portrait contracts rarely terminate", "C": "The entire contract survives because death transfers every personal obligation", "D": "The personal duty does not pass, and the estate cannot demand the remaining fee"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The personal duty does not pass, and the estate cannot demand the remaining fee): article 1441 transmits contractual rights and duties to heirs only when the contract's nature permits it. A fails because a personal artistic performance cannot be replaced freely by the estate. C is the strongest wrong option because not every obligation survives death. The consequence is that the estate cannot force the substitute painter on the client or collect the unearned balance."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "Two separate contractors each leave a hazardous cable across the same public walkway. A cyclist is injured, but the evidence cannot establish which cable caused the fall, although either could have done so. Both contractors deny responsibility and blame the other. What liability principle applies?", "options": {"A": "Each contractor is free because the exact causal cable remains unidentified, under the family rights analysis.", "B": "Only the contractor with the first chronological cable is presumptively liable", "C": "They may be solidarily liable when separate faults could each have caused the injury", "D": "The cyclist must divide the claim equally against each contractor in advance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They may be solidarily liable when separate faults could each have caused the injury): article 1480 imposes solidarity where separate faults may have caused one injury and the actual cause cannot be determined. A fails because uncertainty about the precise causal cable does not automatically free both wrongdoers. B is the strongest wrong option because chronology does not create a statutory presumption of causation. The consequence is that the cyclist may recover from either contractor while contribution is determined between them."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A used piano dealer gives the consumer a written price presentation but omits a structural crack that substantially affects value. The consumer proves the omission occurred in that representation and that the crack was important, then seeks nullity. The dealer argues that the consumer must separately prove the price was inflated. How should the proof issue be analyzed?", "options": {"A": "Once the prohibited practice is proved, the statutory presumption may supply the harmful reliance", "B": "The consumer must generally prove every subjective reason for entering the contract", "C": "An omission can rarely support nullity because silence is legally irrelevant", "D": "The dealer bears the entire burden before the consumer proves any representation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Once the prohibited practice is proved, the statutory presumption may supply the harmful reliance): once a section 228 omission of an important fact in a representation is proved, section 253 presumes that the consumer would not have contracted or would not have paid as high a price. The consumer does not need to prove an independent inflated-price counterfactual or every subjective reason for contracting. The dealer may contest the statutory consequences, but the presumption applies after the representation and omission are established."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A consumer buys goods online and receives the contract copy on 1 February. The copy omits required price information, and the consumer discovers that defect immediately. On 10 February, the consumer sends a cancellation notice relying only on that omission. No later discovery or payment-card trigger applies. Is the distance-contract cancellation timely?", "options": {"A": "No, because the seven-day period had expired before the notice was sent", "B": "Yes, because every distance contract allows thirty days from receipt", "C": "Yes, because missing price information suspends all time limits indefinitely", "D": "No, because distance contracts cannot be cancelled after delivery of a copy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because the seven-day period had expired before the notice was sent): section 54.8 provides a seven-day cancellation period from receipt of the copy when its disclosure or form conditions are breached. B fails because the thirty-day rule concerns failure to send a copy within fifteen days, not the facts here. C is the strongest wrong option because missing information does not suspend time indefinitely. The consequence is that the consumer may pursue other remedies but cannot rely solely on that cancellation trigger."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "An online retailer advertises a $499 total price for an appliance. At checkout, it adds a mandatory $75 handling charge that was absent from the advertisement and cannot be avoided. The consumer completes the purchase and then challenges the difference. Which consumer rule best addresses the problem?", "options": {"A": "The consumer must accept the charge because checkout occurred after the advertisement", "B": "The advertised price controls only when the merchant ships the same day", "C": "The mandatory undisclosed charge may breach the advertised-price prohibition", "D": "Only tax rules apply because every other mandatory charge is unregulated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The mandatory undisclosed charge may breach the advertised-price prohibition): section 224 prohibits charging a price higher than the price advertised, and the advertised price must represent the total amount payable excluding applicable sales taxes. B fails because same-day shipping is irrelevant to the advertised amount. A is the strongest wrong option because a later checkout screen does not cure a mandatory undisclosed charge. The consequence is that the consumer may seek a Consumer Protection Act remedy for the difference and related injury."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A store advertises a popular appliance without saying that only three units are available. Staff know the advertisement will reach thousands of customers and had no reasonable belief of adequate supply. By opening time, the appliance sells out immediately. The store argues that no customer actually bought one. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "No prohibited practice exists because the advertised product was real", "B": "The advertisement may violate the limited-quantity disclosure rule", "C": "The issue concerns only contract formation, not business practices", "D": "The store is protected because stock shortages generally excuse advertising"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The advertisement may violate the limited-quantity disclosure rule): section 231 restricts advertising goods with insufficient quantity unless the limited supply and quantity are disclosed. A fails because the product's existence does not answer the supply representation. D is the strongest wrong option because a shortage is not automatically excused when the advertiser lacked a reasonable belief of adequate supply. The consequence is that the consumer may challenge a prohibited practice even without completing a purchase."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A consumer contract requires all future disputes to be arbitrated. After a disagreement arises, the consumer signs a separate agreement to arbitrate that specific dispute and receives independent time to consider it. The merchant now relies on the second agreement. Is that later agreement effective?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a post-dispute arbitration agreement may be valid", "B": "No, because the original pre-dispute clause lastingly poisons later consent", "C": "No, because consumer contracts can rarely contain or allow arbitration", "D": "Yes, but only if the merchant waives every contractual defence"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because a post-dispute arbitration agreement may be valid): section 11.1 prohibits compulsory pre-dispute arbitration clauses but permits a consumer to agree to arbitration after a dispute arises. C fails because it denies every lawful arbitration possibility. B is the strongest wrong option because a prohibited original clause does not erase independent later consent. The consequence is that the separate post-dispute agreement may govern this specific dispute."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A buyer purchases a device from an unauthorized dealer. The manufacturer's conventional warranty applies only to sales through certified dealers. The unauthorized dealer did not notify the buyer in writing before the sale, and the device later requires warranty repairs. Who must bear that conventional warranty?", "options": {"A": "The buyer, because choosing an unauthorized dealer ends every warranty", "B": "The manufacturer, because dealer status can rarely restrict a conventional warranty", "C": "The unauthorized dealer, because the required written notice was missing", "D": "No one, because conventional warranties cannot apply to electronic devices"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The unauthorized dealer, because the required written notice was missing): section 47 requires an uncertified merchant to warn the consumer in writing that a manufacturer's conditional warranty will not apply, failing which the merchant assumes it. A fails because the dealer's omission protects the buyer. B is the strongest wrong option because the manufacturer may condition its conventional warranty on certified installation or supply. The consequence is that the dealer must honour the warranty at its expense."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A warehouse fire destroys a tenant's goods. The tenant speculates that an old electrical outlet caused it, but the fire expert cannot identify the source and another witness saw an external flare enter the loading bay. The tenant bears the burden of proving a causal link. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The landlord must disprove every possible fire source before avoiding liability", "B": "The expert uncertainty automatically raises a presumption against the landlord", "C": "The tenant must show that the alleged fault more probably caused the fire", "D": "Any possible connection is sufficient because proof beyond doubt is unnecessary"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The tenant must show that the alleged fault more probably caused the fire): articles 2803 and 2804 place the burden on the claimant and apply the balance of probabilities. A fails because the landlord need not disprove every speculative alternative source. B is the strongest wrong option because expert uncertainty alone does not create a presumption of causation. The consequence is that the tenant's claim fails if the evidence leaves the alleged electrical cause no more probable than the external flare."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A retailer advertises a coat at $300 and claims its regular price is $500. Records show the merchant never offered that coat at $500 and created the reference price solely to exaggerate the discount. The consumer buys the coat and later challenges the pricing statement. What rule applies?", "options": {"A": "The reference price is valid because the merchant displayed it prominently", "B": "The claim is valid if another merchant once charged $500 for a similar coat", "C": "The consumer must prove that $300 exceeded the coat's manufacturing cost", "D": "A false reference price may constitute a prohibited commercial practice"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A false reference price may constitute a prohibited commercial practice): section 225 prohibits falsely invoking a reduction or indicating a regular or reference price. A fails because prominent display does not make a false claim lawful. B is the strongest wrong option because another merchant's price does not justify this merchant's invented reference. The consequence is that the consumer may invoke the protections against misleading pricing."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A consumer signs a clause stating that every Consumer Protection Act right is waived and that only the merchant may decide disputes. The merchant later relies on that waiver after delivering defective services. The consumer asks whether a private clause can remove the statutory protections. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The waiver is ineffective because consumers cannot waive the Act's rights", "B": "The waiver is valid because the consumer signed a separate highlighted page", "C": "The waiver is valid if the merchant gave a small discount for it", "D": "The waiver applies only after the consumer first complains in writing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The waiver is ineffective because consumers cannot waive the Act's rights): section 262 prohibits a consumer from waiving rights granted by the Consumer Protection Act unless the Act provides otherwise. B fails because signing or highlighting a waiver does not override that rule. C is the strongest wrong option because a discount cannot purchase a prohibited waiver. The consequence is that the statutory protections remain available despite the clause."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A sales representative signs a consumer contract for goods on a merchant's behalf. The merchant later says the employee exceeded internal signing authority and refuses the contract. The consumer relied on the representative's apparent role, had no notice of any restriction, and paid the first instalment. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The contract is void because internal authority limits bind every consumer", "B": "The consumer must sue only the employee personally", "C": "The merchant becomes bound only if the board ratifies the sale", "D": "The representative's signature can bind the merchant under the Act"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The representative's signature can bind the merchant under the Act): section 31 makes a representative's signature binding on the merchant, while section 10 prevents the merchant from escaping responsibility for its representative's acts. A fails because undisclosed internal authority limits do not automatically defeat the consumer contract. B is the strongest wrong option because the representative's role does not remove the merchant's responsibility. The consequence is that the merchant must honour the signed consumer contract."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A merchant provides a one-month free trial of a service. The contract says the consumer must send a refusal notice before expiry or become liable at the regular price. The merchant never obtains the consumer's explicit post-trial acceptance and later charges the regular monthly fee. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The charge is valid because the trial contract disclosed the renewal term", "B": "The required refusal notice is a prohibited negative-option practice", "C": "The consumer owes the fee because silence generally accepts a service", "D": "Only the financial institution that processed payment may cancel the charge"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The required refusal notice is a prohibited negative-option practice): section 230 prohibits requiring a consumer who received goods or services free for a fixed period to send notice merely to avoid continuing at the regular price. A fails because disclosing the automatic term does not make the practice lawful. C is the strongest wrong option because silence does not create acceptance in this protected setting. The consequence is that the regular-price charge may be challenged and refunded."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A dealer sells a used road vehicle that must pass a mechanical inspection before it may legally travel on public highways. At signing, the dealer provides no inspection certificate and promises one after payment. The buyer later discovers serious defects. What obligation applies at the sale?", "options": {"A": "The buyer must arrange and pay for the required inspection personally", "B": "The dealer must provide the required certificate in the statutory context", "C": "The certificate can be replaced by an oral assurance of roadworthiness", "D": "Inspection is unnecessary whenever the vehicle receives a warranty"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The dealer must provide the required certificate in the statutory context): section 260.28 requires a dealer to give the consumer the mechanical inspection certificate when the Highway Safety Code requires inspection before highway use. A fails because the dealer bears that statutory duty. C is the strongest wrong option because an oral assurance does not satisfy the certificate requirement. The consequence is that the missing certificate exposes the dealer to consumer remedies."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "A retailer advertises a new refrigerator, one of the goods subject to the statutory good-working-order warranty. The advertised price appears prominently, but the required warranty duration is buried in an unrelated webpage. The consumer claims inadequate warranty disclosure near the price. What rule applies?", "options": {"A": "Warranty duration need not appear before the consumer visits a store", "B": "Any webpage statement satisfies the near-price disclosure requirement", "C": "Only the manufacturer may disclose warranty duration near the price", "D": "The merchant must show the statutory warranty duration prominently near the price"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The merchant must show the statutory warranty duration prominently near the price): section 38.8 requires the good-working-order warranty duration to appear prominently near the advertised price or retail value. A fails because this duty reaches advertising before a store visit. B is the strongest wrong option because a buried unrelated webpage does not satisfy the placement and prominence rule. The consequence is that the advertisement must clearly associate the refrigerator's warranty period with its price."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "civil_liability_and_consumer_protection", "question": "An appliance contains a hidden component deliberately designed to stop functioning after eighteen months even though the product is normally expected to last much longer. The manufacturer says the component is part of its ordinary design choice. The consumer challenges the product's limited operating life. What statutory issue arises?", "options": {"A": "A short conventional warranty makes the design automatically lawful, under the Quebec consumer context.", "B": "Planned obsolescence through a technique reducing normal life is prohibited", "C": "Consumers must prove the manufacturer intended direct personal injury", "D": "Only unsafe products, not short-lived products, fall within the Act"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Planned obsolescence through a technique reducing normal life is prohibited): section 227.0.4 prohibits carrying on business in goods whose obsolescence is planned through a technique intended to shorten normal operating life. A fails because a short conventional warranty does not authorize that design. C is the strongest wrong option because physical injury is not required for the obsolescence prohibition. The consequence is that the manufacturer may face consumer-law remedies for the hidden limiting component."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A consumer living in Sherbrooke sues a Montreal merchant over an ordinary consumer contract. The defendant wants the case moved to Montreal because that is where the merchant operates. The claim arose from that consumer relationship and no special rule sends it elsewhere. Which district may hear the case?", "options": {"A": "Only Montreal because the merchant's establishment controls every case", "B": "Only the district where the contract was first drafted", "C": "The consumer may proceed in the district of their residence", "D": "The court clerk chooses between Montreal and Sherbrooke"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The consumer may proceed in the district of their residence): article 43 gives the court of the consumer's domicile or residence jurisdiction in a consumer contract. A fails because the merchant's establishment is not the only available forum. B is the strongest wrong option because drafting location does not replace the consumer-specific rule. The consequence is that Sherbrooke may hear the case."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "After filing a defence, a defendant first argues that the court entirely lacks subject-matter jurisdiction. The objection appears after the case protocol and several hearings, and the plaintiff says it is too late. The court also doubts its own authority to decide the dispute. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The objection is waived once the defendant files any defence", "B": "Only the plaintiff may ask the court to transfer the case", "C": "The case protocol lastingly cures a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction", "D": "The court may raise subject-matter jurisdiction at any stage itself"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court may raise subject-matter jurisdiction at any stage itself): article 167 permits a party to raise lack of subject-matter jurisdiction at any stage and allows the court to declare it on its own initiative. A fails because filing a defence does not waive a fundamental jurisdictional defect. B is the strongest wrong option because the rule does not restrict the issue to the plaintiff. The consequence is that the case may be dismissed or transferred when the court lacks authority."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "In a contract dispute where the amount claimed is $40,000, one party includes an oral pre-trial examination in the case protocol. The opposing party objects and asks the court to authorize the examination despite the low amount. The evidence is relevant but not at risk of disappearing. What rule governs?", "options": {"A": "A pre-trial examination is available whenever the protocol mentions it", "B": "Pre-trial examination is not permitted where the amount claimed is below $50,000", "C": "The requesting party must show that the witness will otherwise refuse testimony", "D": "The court may authorize the examination solely to reduce trial length"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pre-trial examination is not permitted where the amount claimed is below $50,000): article 229 sets that threshold, and a relevant protocol entry does not override it. A fails because inclusion in the protocol cannot authorize a statutorily barred examination. C is the strongest wrong option because the rule applies by amount and does not require threatened refusal. The consequence is that the $40,000 case must proceed without a pre-trial examination."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A money judgment is rendered with provisional execution expressly ordered despite any appeal. The defendant files a valid appeal and asks the bailiff to stay all execution automatically. The plaintiff wants to enforce the judgment while the appeal proceeds. What result follows?", "options": {"A": "The appeal automatically erases every provisional execution order, under the recorded contractual setting.", "B": "The defendant's appeal prevents every execution measure generally", "C": "The judgment may be executed despite the appeal because provisional execution was ordered", "D": "Execution continues only if the defendant posts no security"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The judgment may be executed despite the appeal because provisional execution was ordered): article 355 stays execution after a properly initiated appeal except where provisional execution has been ordered or is provided by law. A fails because an appeal does not automatically cancel that order. B is the strongest wrong option because it ignores the express exception. The consequence is that the plaintiff may continue execution during the appeal."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A creditor obtains a seizure before judgment of the defendant's equipment based on an affidavit. Within five days after service, the defendant argues that key factual allegations in the affidavit are false. No other exceptional delay applies. What procedural remedy is available?", "options": {"A": "The defendant must wait until final judgment to contest the seizure", "B": "The defendant may appeal before asking the court to review the affidavit", "C": "Only the bailiff may decide whether the seizure should continue, under the recorded family context.", "D": "The defendant may ask the court to quash the seizure within that five-day period"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The defendant may ask the court to quash the seizure within that five-day period): article 522 allows the defendant, within five days after service, to contest a seizure before judgment on the ground that the affidavit is insufficient or false. A fails because waiting until final judgment would defeat the provisional remedy. B is the strongest wrong option because a prompt first-instance contest need not begin with an appeal. The consequence is that the court decides whether to quash or confirm the seizure."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "After judgment, a seizing creditor serves the notice of execution on a garnishee who owes money to the debtor. The garnishee neither declares the debt nor contests the notice within the prescribed delay. The creditor asks what default period applies to the declaration. What rule governs?", "options": {"A": "The garnishee must declare the debt to the bailiff within ten days", "B": "The garnishee may wait until final distribution of the proceeds", "C": "The garnishee has one year because the debt belongs to the debtor", "D": "The declaration deadline depends mainly on the debtor's preference"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The garnishee must declare the debt to the bailiff within ten days): article 711 requires the garnishee to declare the amount, cause, and terms of the debt within ten days after service. B fails because declaration occurs before distribution. C is the strongest wrong option because the one-year period is unrelated. The consequence is that missing the ten-day period can trigger consequences against the garnishee."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A journalist seeks access to a family case record and asks to attend a first-instance family hearing. The matter concerns a child's family situation, and no court order permits public access or disclosure of identifying information. What access rules apply?", "options": {"A": "The journalist may publish all identifying details because hearings are public", "B": "The hearing is held in camera and access to the record is restricted", "C": "Access depends mainly on the parties' lawyers granting permission", "D": "The record becomes public whenever a judgment is eventually issued"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The hearing is held in camera and access to the record is restricted): articles 15 and 16 protect first-instance family hearings and restrict access to family records and identifying information. A fails because the ordinary public-hearing principle yields to these specific protections. C is the strongest wrong option because lawyers do not control court-record access. The consequence is that the journalist cannot publish identifying details without judicial authority."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "During trial, a judge notices facts suggesting an additional remedy that neither party requested or included in the pleadings. The judge wants to award that remedy because it appears fair and efficient. The defendant has had no notice that the extra conclusion was in issue. What procedural limit applies?", "options": {"A": "The court cannot adjudicate beyond the subject matter and conclusions of the parties", "B": "The court may award any remedy it considers socially useful", "C": "The court may amend the successful party's pleading after judgment", "D": "The court may decide the new remedy if the clerk announces it first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court cannot adjudicate beyond the subject matter and conclusions of the parties): article 10 confines adjudication to what is sought and requires parties to define the proceeding. B fails because judicial power is not a general authority to create socially useful remedies. C is the strongest wrong option because a post-judgment amendment would still deprive the defendant of a fair opportunity to debate the new claim. The consequence is that any additional remedy requires a properly connected amendment and fair notice before adjudication."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A judgment correctly decides liability but contains an obvious clerical error in the property description and accidentally omits one requested conclusion. The judge who rendered it remains available, and the error is evident from the record. What correction mechanism applies?", "options": {"A": "The winning party must begin a substantially new action", "B": "Only the Court of Appeal may correct any error in a trial judgment", "C": "The judge may correct the clerical error or obvious inadvertent omission", "D": "The court clerk may rewrite the operative part without judicial involvement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The judge may correct the clerical error or obvious inadvertent omission): article 338 permits correction of written or calculation errors, property-description mistakes, awards beyond the pleadings, and omitted conclusions caused by obvious inadvertence. A fails because a new action is unnecessary for a correctable record error. B is the strongest wrong option because clerical correction begins in the court that rendered judgment. The consequence is that the judge may amend the judgment consistently with the record."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "During execution, a bailiff believes force may be necessary to enter a locked building and seize property. The debtor refuses access, and the bailiff wants to break the lock immediately without any further authorization. What procedural requirement applies before forced entry?", "options": {"A": "The creditor may authorize entry through a private email", "B": "The bailiff may enter forcibly whenever the judgment is final", "C": "A neighbour must witness the entry and sign the bailiff's minutes", "D": "The bailiff must obtain authorization from the special clerk of the district"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The bailiff must obtain authorization from the special clerk of the district): article 686 requires special-clerk authorization before forced entry for seizure, removal, or eviction. A fails because the creditor cannot authorize force by email. B is the strongest wrong option because a final judgment alone does not permit every forced entry. The consequence is that the bailiff must obtain the required authorization before breaking the lock."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A commercial arbitration clause broadly covers every disagreement, including a party's legal status and family capacity. One party now tries to submit a status dispute to the arbitrator under the broad wording. What limitation on the arbitration agreement applies under current Quebec procedural law?", "options": {"A": "A broad clause includes every subject despite public order, under the offered factual circumstances.", "B": "Status, capacity, family, and other public order disputes cannot be arbitrated", "C": "The arbitrator may decide status matters after notifying the parties", "D": "The dispute becomes arbitrable once the parties sign an additional waiver"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Status, capacity, family, and other public order disputes cannot be arbitrated): article 2639 of the Civil Code excludes those matters from arbitration despite broad contractual wording. A fails because private wording cannot extend arbitration into excluded public-order subjects. C is the strongest wrong option because the arbitrator lacks jurisdiction to decide them. The consequence is that the status dispute must proceed before the competent court or authority."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "related_civil_procedure", "question": "A natural person in a small-claims case wants a non-remunerated friend to help present the claim. The friend is not a lawyer, but the person will provide a signed mandate explaining why they cannot self-represent. The opposing party objects to any non-lawyer participation. What rule applies?", "options": {"A": "A non-lawyer friend may rarely speak in a court proceeding", "B": "A natural person may mandate a non-remunerated relative or friend in small claims", "C": "Only a professional-order member may assist a self-represented person", "D": "The friend must first obtain the defendant's written consent, under the offered client situation."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A natural person may mandate a non-remunerated relative or friend in small claims): article 542 permits self-representation and a documented non-remunerated mandate to a spouse, relative, connected person, or friend in the small-claims framework. A fails because the Code creates that limited exception. C is the strongest wrong option because professional membership is not required for the mandated friend. The consequence is that the signed mandate may allow the friend to assist in presenting the claim."}];
